-- decommissioning-provision-psak-237-and-contingencies
UPDATE public.essays
   SET content = $c$<p><strong>In brief</strong></p><ul><li><p>A provision is a liability of uncertain timing or amount. It is recognised when a past event has left a present obligation the entity cannot realistically avoid, an outflow is more likely than not, and the amount can be estimated. Anything weaker is a contingent liability, disclosed unless remote.</p></li><li><p>A provision is measured at the best estimate of what it would rationally cost to settle the obligation today. That is an expected value over a large population or the most likely outcome adjusted for skew for a single obligation, discounted at a pre-tax rate that matches the cash flows (nominal with nominal, real with real).</p></li><li><p>A decommissioning obligation is a provision whose cost is added to the asset that caused it. The discount unwinds as a finance cost, and changes in the estimate or the rate move the asset, not profit, until the asset is used up. PHE carries 3,826,222 of it, its largest liability.</p></li></ul><hr><p>PT Pertamina Hulu Energi and subsidiaries (PHE) report a provision for decommissioning and site restoration of 3,826,222 at 31 December 2024 against 3,784,917 a year earlier, in USD thousand (PHE FS 2024, statement of financial position, Daftar 1/2, PDF p. 12; "Daftar" is the printed page). It is the largest liability line, 25.0 percent of total liabilities and 12.6 percent of total assets (22.7 and 12.3 percent a year earlier). This page also covers other non-current liabilities, 281,228 (273,774 in 2023).</p><p>A student should come away able to tell a provision from a contingent liability, measure it, journal a decommissioning provision through its life, and see where other standards take over.</p><p>All amounts are in USD thousand unless stated. Figures from PHE's statements are cited to their note. Every other number is labelled an illustration.</p><h2>1. What a provision is</h2><p>A provision is a liability whose timing or amount is uncertain (PSAK 237.10; formerly PSAK 57, IAS 37). It is still a liability, a present obligation from a past event settled by an outflow of resources. The uncertainty sets it apart from other liabilities (para. 11).</p><table style="min-width: 100px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Trade payables</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Accruals</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Provisions</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Goods or services</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>received</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>received</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>often none involved</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Amount and date</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>invoiced or formally agreed</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>estimated, with little uncertainty</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>uncertain in timing or amount</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>PHE example, 2024</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>trade payables 1,091,822</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>accrued expenses 1,897,743</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>this line, 3,826,222</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Allowances against assets, such as PHE's "provision for impairment of non-financial assets", are not PSAK 237 provisions (para. 7). A provision is current or non-current by expected settlement date; PHE presents this one as wholly non-current.</p><h2>2. Recognition</h2><p>A provision is recognised only when three conditions hold together (para. 14): a present obligation, legal or constructive, from a past event; a probable outflow of resources; and a reliable estimate.</p><p><strong>Present obligation from a past event.</strong> The past event is the <em>obligating event</em>, one that leaves the entity no realistic alternative to settling (para. 17). A <em>legal obligation</em> comes from contract or law; a <em>constructive obligation</em> from the entity's own policy or practice that has created a valid expectation in others (para. 10).</p><p>The test that does most of the work is whether the obligation exists independently of the entity's future actions (para. 19). The standard's own example is upstream. Decommissioning an oil installation is provided for to the extent damage has already been done, while a cost the entity could avoid by operating differently, such as fitting new filters, is not. For example (illustration), a subsidiary that has drilled 12 wells and plans 8 more provides for plugging 12; the 8 could still go undrilled.</p><p>The same logic rules out provisions for future operating losses (para. 63); an expected loss is an impairment indicator instead (para. 65).</p><p><strong>Probable outflow.</strong> Probable means more likely than not, a probability above 50 percent (para. 23). For many similar obligations, such as warranties, the test is applied to the class as a whole (para. 24).</p><p><strong>Reliable estimate.</strong> The standard expects a range of outcomes to be estimable in all but extremely rare cases (para. 25). Where none can be made, the liability is disclosed as a contingent liability (para. 26).</p><h2>3. Measurement</h2><p>The amount recognised is the <em>best estimate</em> of the expenditure required to settle the obligation at the reporting date (para. 36). That is the amount the entity would rationally pay to settle it, or to transfer it to a third party, today (para. 37).</p><p><strong>Expected value for a large population.</strong> When the provision covers many items, outcomes are weighted by their probabilities (para. 39). For example (illustration), a subsidiary must plug 40 old onshore wells. Experience says 70 percent need a simple cement plug at 400 each, 25 percent need remedial cementing at 700, and 5 percent have stuck pipe that must be fished out at 1,600.</p><blockquote><p>Expected cost per well = 70% × 400 + 25% × 700 + 5% × 1,600 = 280 + 175 + 80 = 535</p><p>Provision, before discounting = 40 × 535 = 21,400</p></blockquote><p><strong>Most likely outcome for a single obligation.</strong> One platform has one outcome, so the starting point is the most likely one. Other outcomes still count. If they are mostly higher, the best estimate is higher (para. 40). For example (illustration), removing one platform will most likely cost 30,000 (60 percent), but there is a 30 percent chance of 45,000 if the jacket is corroded and a 10 percent chance of 25,000 if a heavy-lift vessel is already in the area.</p><blockquote><p>Most likely outcome = 30,000; probability-weighted outcome = 60% × 30,000 + 30% × 45,000 + 10% × 25,000 = 34,000</p></blockquote><p>The skew is upward, so the best estimate lies above 30,000.</p><p><strong>Risk.</strong> Uncertainty is reflected once, in the cash flows or the rate, never both (paras. 42–43 and 47).</p><p><strong>Present value.</strong> Where timing matters, the provision is the present value of the expected outflows, at a pre-tax rate reflecting current market assessments of the time value of money and the risks specific to the liability (paras. 45–47). Cash flows and rate must match. Suppose the 34,000 is in today's prices, the work happens in 10 years, cost inflation is 2.5 percent and the nominal rate is 5.0 percent.</p><blockquote><p>Nominal method: 34,000 × 1.025^10 = 43,523 in year-10 prices; 43,523 / 1.05^10 = 26,719</p><p>Real method: real rate = 1.05 / 1.025 − 1 = 2.439%; 34,000 / 1.02439^10 = 26,719</p><p>Mixed (today's prices at the nominal rate): 34,000 / 1.05^10 = 20,873, understated by 5,846</p></blockquote><p><strong>Future events, disposals and reimbursements.</strong> Expected technology savings count only on objective evidence, new legislation only when virtually certain (paras. 48–50), and scrap proceeds not at all (para. 51). A reimbursement, for example from an insurer, is a separate asset recognised only when virtually certain and capped at the provision (para. 53); only the expense may be shown net (para. 54).</p><h2>4. Common provisions</h2><p>The lecture notes list six forms. Litigation, warranties, onerous contracts and restructuring follow, environmental obligations get section 5, and consideration payable has left PSAK 237.</p><p><strong>Litigation.</strong> A lawsuit needs a cause before the reporting date, a probable loss and an estimate; expert opinion is evidence (para. 16).</p><p><strong>Warranties.</strong> An <em>assurance-type warranty</em> promises that the product works as specified, so it is a cost of the sale, provided for when the sale is made. A <em>service-type warranty</em> sells extra cover, a separate performance obligation under PSAK 115 (see the Deferred revenue essay). In the notes' example a seller sells 1,000 units at USD 500 each, expects warranty repairs on 100 units at USD 200 each (full amounts), pays 20 claims in the year and expects 80 next year.</p><blockquote><p>Paid in the year: Dr Warranty expense 4,000 / Cr Cash, inventories 4,000</p><p>31 December: Dr Warranty expense 16,000 / Cr Warranty liability 16,000 (80 × 200)</p></blockquote><p><em>Consideration payable</em> to customers, such as premiums and rebates, is now part of revenue under PSAK 115. It reduces the transaction price, and an expected refund is a refund liability (see the Deferred revenue essay).</p><p><strong>Onerous contracts.</strong> A contract is <em>onerous</em> when the unavoidable cost of meeting it exceeds the benefit expected from it (para. 10), and the unavoidable cost is the <em>least net cost of exiting</em>, the lower of the cost of fulfilling it net of what fulfilling still earns and the penalty for walking away (para. 68). Assets dedicated to the contract are tested for impairment first (para. 69). Since the 2020 amendment the cost of fulfilling includes both incremental costs and an allocation of other directly related costs. For example (illustration), a subsidiary has a drilling services contract costing 60,000 for 2025, and the programme is cancelled. Exiting costs a penalty of 25,000; re-selling the services to another operator would bring in 40,000.</p><blockquote><p>Cost of fulfilling, net of benefits = 60,000 − 40,000 = 20,000; penalty = 25,000</p><p>Provision = lower of the two = 20,000</p><p>Dr Loss on onerous contract 20,000 / Cr Provision for onerous contract 20,000</p></blockquote><p><strong>Restructuring.</strong> A constructive obligation needs a detailed formal plan, started or announced to those affected (para. 72). Only direct costs not associated with continuing activities are included, so retraining staff who stay, marketing and new systems are excluded (paras. 80–81).</p><h2>5. Decommissioning: the asset retirement obligation</h2><p>Installing a well, platform or pipeline creates an obligation to plug, remove and restore the site at the end of field life. PHE recognises it for "oil and gas production facilities, wells, pipelines and related assets in accordance with the provisions in the PSC or in line with applicable regulations" (Note 3o, Daftar 5/60, PDF p. 77). The Oil and gas properties essay shows the asset side; this section follows the liability.</p><p><strong>Initial recognition.</strong> The provision is recognised on installation and grows as more wells are drilled. The cost goes into the asset under PSAK 216.16(c) (formerly PSAK 16, IAS 16), because removing the platform is part of what it costs to use it. The removal cost has no future benefit of its own, so it is not a separate asset (the lecture notes, following Kieso). Where the obligation arises from producing inventory during a period, as when a mine disturbs more land to extract this year's ore, the cost goes to inventory under PSAK 202 instead.</p><p>For example (illustration), a PHE subsidiary completes an offshore platform with 20 wells on 31 December 2024. Removal and plugging are expected at the end of 2036, 12 years later. In today's prices the engineers see a 60 percent chance of 80,000, 30 percent of 100,000 and 10 percent of 140,000. Cost inflation is 2.5 percent and the pre-tax nominal discount rate 5.0 percent.</p><blockquote><p>Expected cost in today's prices = 60% × 80,000 + 30% × 100,000 + 10% × 140,000 = 92,000</p><p>In 2036 prices = 92,000 × 1.025^12 = 123,730</p><p>Present value at 31 December 2024 = 123,730 / 1.05^12 = 68,897</p></blockquote><p>One platform is a single obligation, so 80,000 is the most likely outcome. The other outcomes are all higher, so the best estimate lies above it, and this page uses the weighted 92,000, as section 3 allowed.</p><blockquote><p>Dr Oil and gas properties 68,897 / Cr Provision for decommissioning 68,897</p></blockquote><p><strong>Unwinding of the discount.</strong> As settlement approaches, the present value rises by the discount rate. The increase is a <em>finance cost</em> (para. 60) that ISAK 101 (formerly ISAK 9, IFRIC 1) sends to profit or loss and forbids capitalising. PHE calls it accretion.</p><blockquote><p>2025: 68,897 × 5% = 3,445; provision = 72,342</p><p>2026: 72,342 × 5% = 3,617; provision = 75,959</p></blockquote><blockquote><p>Dr Finance cost 3,445 / Cr Provision for decommissioning 3,445 (2026: 3,617)</p></blockquote><p><strong>Changes in estimate.</strong> The expected cash flows, their timing and the discount rate all move the provision. Under ISAK 101, for an asset carried at cost, the change is added to or deducted from the asset and depleted over its remaining life. A deduction may not exceed the asset's carrying amount, and any excess goes to profit. An addition is considered as a possible impairment indicator and, if it is one, the asset is tested. Once the asset reaches the end of its useful life, every later change goes to profit or loss. PHE's policy states the add/deduct rule, the cap and the impairment check (Note 3o).</p><p>Suppose that at the end of 2026 the market rate for this liability falls to 4.0 percent, with 10 years to go and the cash estimate unchanged.</p><blockquote><p>Remeasured provision = 123,730 / 1.04^10 = 83,588; increase = 83,588 − 75,959 = 7,629</p><p>Dr Oil and gas properties 7,629 / Cr Provision for decommissioning 7,629</p></blockquote><p>From 2027 the provision unwinds at 4.0 percent. Now suppose that at the end of 2033, with three years left, a cheaper plugging method cuts the 2036 estimate by 12,000, and the platform's carrying amount is down to 6,000.</p><blockquote><p>Provision before the change = 123,730 / 1.04^3 = 109,996</p><p>Decrease = 12,000 / 1.04^3 = 10,668; new provision = 99,328</p><p>Dr Provision for decommissioning 10,668 / Cr Oil and gas properties 6,000 / Cr Gain on decommissioning estimate 4,668</p></blockquote><p><strong>Settlement.</strong> The provision is used only for its original purpose (para. 61). By the end of 2036 the provision has unwound to the revised nominal estimate of 123,730 − 12,000 = 111,730. If the contractor charges 115,000, the 3,270 difference is a loss of that period.</p><blockquote><p>Dr Provision for decommissioning 111,730, Dr Loss on settlement 3,270 / Cr Cash 115,000</p></blockquote><p><strong>Sensitivity.</strong> A long-dated provision moves sharply with its discount rate. Take PHE's 2024 balance as the starting point, and treat it, for illustration only, as one payment 15 years away at 3.17 percent, the ratio of PHE's 2024 accretion to its opening balance (Note 29).</p><blockquote><p>Implied payment in 15 years = 3,826,222 × 1.0317^15 = 6,110,428</p><p>At 4.17%: 6,110,428 / 1.0417^15 = 3,310,790, a fall of 515,432 (13.5%)</p><p>At 2.17%: 6,110,428 / 1.0217^15 = 4,428,134, a rise of 601,912 (15.7%)</p></blockquote><p>The rise exceeds the fall because present value is convex in the rate. Under ISAK 101 most of either swing would land on oil and gas properties, not profit.</p><h2>6. Contingent liabilities and contingent assets</h2><p>A <em>contingent liability</em> is either a possible obligation whose existence will be confirmed only by uncertain future events outside the entity's control, or a present obligation that fails the probability or estimate test (para. 10). It is never recognised (para. 27). It is disclosed, with an estimate of its effect where practicable, unless remote (paras. 28 and 86), and becomes a provision in the period the outflow turns probable (para. 30).</p><p><strong>Joint and several liability.</strong> The part of a joint obligation expected to be met by the others is a contingent liability, and the entity provides only for its own probable share (para. 29). For example (illustration), a PHE subsidiary holds 30 percent of a block whose partners are jointly and severally liable for a 10,000 remediation order.</p><blockquote><p>Provision = 30% × 10,000 = 3,000; contingent liability disclosed = 7,000, unless the partners look unable to pay</p></blockquote><p>PHE discloses a real case. A 2022 ICC award orders Pertamina E&amp;P Libya and Pertamina, jointly and severally, to pay Libya's national oil company 24,000 and 5,200 plus interest for unfulfilled exploration commitments (Note 45e(i), Daftar 5/197–5/198, PDF pp. 214–215).</p><p>PHE is also guarantor for a former Australian subsidiary's share of abandonment costs in the Basker Manta Gummy block. The operator sued in Victoria; PHE's position, on legal advice, is that it owes nothing because abandonment was not completed within five years of withdrawal (Note 45e(ii), Daftar 5/198–5/202, PDF pp. 215–219). The note discloses no amount and does not say whether a provision is held. Its place under Note 45, "commitments and contingencies", with no matching provision in Notes 28 or 29, is consistent with a contingent liability.</p><p>A <em>contingent asset</em> is a possible asset from past events, such as a claim being pursued in court (paras. 10 and 32). It is not recognised (paras. 31 and 33), is disclosed when an inflow is probable (para. 34), and is recognised only when virtually certain (para. 35). A liability is booked at more likely than not, an asset only at virtually certain. For example (illustration), an insurance claim of 5,000 disputed at year end is disclosed; when the insurer accepts it in writing next year, it is booked.</p><blockquote><p>Dr Insurance claim receivable 5,000 / Cr Other income 5,000</p></blockquote><h2>7. Decommissioning funds and the other non-current liabilities</h2><p><strong>Funds set aside.</strong> Under IFRIC 5 a contributor to a decommissioning fund keeps its obligation as a liability and recognises its interest in the fund separately; without control or influence over the fund, the reimbursement right is measured at the lower of the provision and its share of the fair value of the fund's net assets. IAI's renumbering list has no ISAK for IFRIC 5, but PSAK 237.53 and the offsetting ban in PSAK 201 (formerly PSAK 1, IAS 1) give the same gross answer. For example (illustration), a provision of 10,000 and a fund of 6,000 appear as a liability of 10,000 and an asset of 6,000, never a net liability of 4,000.</p><p>PHE's funds sit in joint accounts with SKK Migas, usable only for restoration with its approval, and are presented as restricted cash (Note 3o, Daftar 5/61, PDF p. 78), 1,890,493 non-current at the end of 2024 (Note 7, Daftar 5/93–5/95, PDF pp. 110–112), plus its share of joint-operation funds, 66,719, in other non-current assets (Note 14b, Daftar 5/105, PDF p. 122). Their fall from 296,555 is mostly a transfer into PHR's joint account (p. 123).</p><p><strong>Other non-current liabilities.</strong> PHE's Note 28 shows why a liability with "contingent" in its name need not be a PSAK 237 item (Daftar 5/144, PDF p. 161).</p><table style="min-width: 100px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2024</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2023</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Governing standard</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Acquisition of the Masela block</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>167,902</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>159,311</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>PSAK 103, contingent consideration</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Penalty for unfulfilled work commitments (Libya)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>39,600</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>39,600</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>liability; possibly the ICC award in Note 45e(i), not stated</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Monitoring and recovery of two wells</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>25,948</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>25,959</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>PSAK 237, decommissioning-type</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Utilisation of the Badak LNG plant</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>18,313</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>22,503</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>obligation under PHI's WBX-NR sales contract; standard not stated</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>PSC partner</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>10,387</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>9,100</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>not explained</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Others</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>19,078</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>17,312</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>includes Simenggaris sunk cost 9,720 and abandonment costs 7,530</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>Total</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>281,228</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>273,774</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>PHE Masela bought a 20 percent participating interest from Shell, completed on 18 October 2023 (Note 5f, Daftar 5/88, PDF p. 105), and owes a further payment if the block's partners take a final investment decision (FID). Under PSAK 103 <em>Business Combinations</em> (formerly PSAK 22, IFRS 3) contingent consideration is part of the price, recognised at fair value at the acquisition date (PHE measures it at present value under PSAK 113, Note 28) and, if it is a liability, remeasured at fair value through profit or loss (Note 3d, Daftar 5/36, PDF p. 53). PHE applies PSAK 103 to this acquired interest in a joint operation, consistent with PSAK 111's rule for interests that are businesses. PSAK 237 does not apply, both because another standard covers it (para. 5) and because a contractual obligation to pay cash is a financial liability (para. 2).</p><p>The two standards treat the same odds differently. For example (illustration), an acquirer will pay 100,000 if FID is taken in four years, judged 40 percent likely, with a discount rate of 8 percent.</p><blockquote><p>Under PSAK 237 logic: outflow 40% &lt; 50%, nothing recognised, contingent liability disclosed</p><p>Under PSAK 103: fair value = 40% × 100,000 / 1.08^4 = 29,401, recognised as part of the cost of the acquisition</p><p>A year later the odds rise to 60%: 60% × 100,000 / 1.08^3 = 47,630; Dr Loss on remeasurement 18,229 / Cr Contingent consideration liability 18,229</p></blockquote><h2>8. Provisions across industries</h2><p><strong>Mining.</strong> Indonesian miners provide for reclamation and post-mining closure under PP 78/2010 and also place guarantees, such as time deposits, that do not discharge the obligation, so both are shown gross. Power plants carry the same provision. Mining also tests section 5's asset-or-inventory split every year. For example (illustration), a coal pit incurs a restoration obligation of 2,000 for land disturbed to extract this year's coal and 3,000 for a haul road built to serve the mine for ten years.</p><blockquote><p>Dr Inventories, coal 2,000 / Cr Provision for reclamation 2,000</p><p>Dr Mine properties 3,000 / Cr Provision for reclamation 3,000</p></blockquote><p><strong>Lessees and telecom towers.</strong> Restoring leased land puts the cost into the right-of-use asset and the obligation into a PSAK 237 provision (see the Right-of-use essay).</p><p><strong>Levies.</strong> ISAK 121 <em>Levies</em> (formerly ISAK 30, IFRIC 21) makes the obligating event the activity that triggers payment under the law, not the period the levy relates to. For example (illustration), a levy of 1,200 for a year that is triggered by being in operation on 1 January is a full liability of 1,200 on 1 January, not 100 a month.</p><p><strong>Banks and insurers.</strong> Financial guarantees and loan commitments fall under PSAK 109 (formerly PSAK 71, IFRS 9), with expected credit losses, not under PSAK 237 (para. 2). Insurers' claims liabilities fall under PSAK 117 (formerly PSAK 74, IFRS 17).</p><h2>9. Presentation and analysis: PHE's Note 29</h2><p>PSAK 237 asks for a reconciliation showing additions, amounts used, unused amounts reversed and the unwinding (para. 84), and for the expected timing, uncertainties, major assumptions and reimbursements (para. 85). PHE gives a three-line movement (Note 29, Daftar 5/145, PDF p. 162).</p><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2024</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2023</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Balance at beginning of year</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>3,784,917</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>3,460,330</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Additions/(deductions) and revisions of estimates</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>78,620</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>211,366</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Accretion expense</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>119,925</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>113,221</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>Balance at end of year</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>3,826,222</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>3,784,917</strong></p></td></tr></tbody></table><blockquote><p>2023: 3,460,330 + 211,366 + 113,221 = 3,784,917</p><p>2024: 3,784,917 + 78,620 + 119,925 = 3,983,462, not 3,826,222; 3,784,917 − 78,620 + 119,925 = 3,826,222</p></blockquote><p>The 2024 revisions only foot as a deduction of 78,620, though they are printed without brackets. Alternatively PHE omitted a line: if 78,620 is an addition, an unprinted reduction of 157,240 is needed. The Oil and gas properties essay gives the same two readings. Three more points stand out.</p><ul><li><p>There is no line for amounts used. Either no abandonment spending was charged in two years, or it is netted inside the revisions, though paragraph 84 asks for it separately.</p></li><li><p>Note 51 reports non-cash decommissioning adjustments to oil and gas properties of +12,627 in 2024 and 202,685 in 2023 (Daftar 5/235, PDF p. 252), against Note 29 revisions of 78,620 (a deduction on the footing reading) and 211,366. The gaps of 91,247 and 8,681 could be utilisation, revisions taken to profit, disposals or exchange differences; the FS do not say.</p></li><li><p>The discount rate, inflation assumption and timing are not disclosed. Note 29 describes ARO as the present value of costs "expected to be incurred during the terms of the PSCs" and Note 4b(ii) lists the main uncertainties (Daftar 5/80, PDF p. 97).</p></li></ul><blockquote><p>Accretion / opening provision = 119,925 / 3,784,917 = 3.17% (2023: 113,221 / 3,460,330 = 3.27%)</p><p>Accretion / finance cost = 119,925 / 453,906 = 26.4% (Note 41, Daftar 5/150, PDF p. 167)</p><p>Funds held / provision = (1,890,493 + 66,719) / 3,826,222 = 51.2% (2023: 52.8%)</p><p>Deferred tax on the provision = 66,352 + 412,586 = 478,938, or 12.5% of the provision (Note 43e, Daftar 5/155, PDF p. 172)</p></blockquote><p>About half the obligation, at present value, is backed by cash usable for nothing else. The deferred tax ratio is far below PHE's tax rates, plausibly because ASR deposits are recoverable or deductible when placed (section 10), leaving much of the provision with no future deduction; the mechanics belong to the income tax essays. Note 28's 2023 lines also add to 273,785, not the 273,774 printed, a difference of 11.</p><h2>10. Upstream note: abandonment and site restoration under the PSC</h2><p>Indonesian upstream contractors set aside abandonment and site restoration (ASR) funds under Minister of Energy and Mineral Resources Regulation 15/2018, starting in the year production becomes commercial, in a joint account with SKK Migas at a state-owned bank. Under cost recovery the amounts set aside are recoverable costs; under gross split they are deductible for income tax. PHE OSES, for example, deposited funds for both halves of 2024 on SKK Migas instruction (Note 45i, Daftar 5/208, PDF p. 225).</p><h2>11. Related lines</h2><ul><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/oil-gas-properties-net">oil-gas-properties-net</a>: the asset that absorbs the initial estimate and later revisions.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/restricted-cash">restricted-cash-non-current</a>: the ASR funds.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/advances-prepayments-current">other-non-current-assets</a>: funds in joint operations.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/finance-cost">finance-cost</a>: the accretion.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/deferred-tax-assets">deferred-tax-liabilities</a>: the deductible temporary difference on the provision.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/trade-payables">trade-payables</a>, <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/accrued-expenses">accrued-expenses</a>: the liabilities with certain or nearly certain amounts.</p></li><li><p><code>other-non-current-liabilities</code>: covered here, section 7.</p></li></ul><h3>Least-confident assertions</h3><ul><li><p>That PHE treats the Basker Manta Gummy claim as a contingent liability is inferred from its place in Note 45 (commitments and contingencies) and the absence of an amount or provision. The item itself is not labelled.</p></li><li><p>Reading Note 29's 78,620 as a deduction rests on footing alone; PHE may instead have omitted a line of 157,240. The reasons for the gaps to Note 51 are not disclosed, and the listed causes are possibilities, not findings. That ASR deposits explain the low deferred tax ratio is this page's reading.</p></li><li><p>The 3.17 percent "rate" and the single 15-year payment in the sensitivity are illustrative simplifications. PHE discloses neither its discount rate nor the timing, and a portfolio of fields with different rates and dates would respond differently.</p></li><li><p>That Indonesia has no ISAK equivalent of IFRIC 5 rests on IAI's renumbering list, which lists only interpretations in force. Whether PHE's joint ASR accounts would fall within IFRIC 5's scope if it applied is this page's reading; PHE presents them as restricted cash.</p></li><li><p>The governing standards given for the Note 28 items are this page's reading of one-line descriptions. The Libya penalty may be the ICC award of Note 45e(i); the FS do not link them, and the balance shows no interest accrual. The 51.2 percent funding ratio assumes all non-current restricted cash is ASR funds, as Note 7's description implies but does not state line by line.</p></li></ul><h2>References</h2><ol><li><p>PT Pertamina Hulu Energi dan Entitas Anak, consolidated financial statements 31 December 2024 (audited, USD thousand): statement of financial position (Daftar 1/2, PDF p. 12); Note 3d (Daftar 5/36, PDF p. 53); Note 3o (Daftar 5/60–5/61, PDF pp. 77–78); Note 4b(ii) (Daftar 5/80, PDF p. 97); Note 5f (Daftar 5/88, PDF p. 105); Note 7 (Daftar 5/93–5/95, PDF pp. 110–112); Note 14b (Daftar 5/105–5/106, PDF pp. 122–123); Note 28 (Daftar 5/144, PDF p. 161); Note 29 (Daftar 5/145, PDF p. 162); Note 41 (Daftar 5/150, PDF p. 167); Note 43e (Daftar 5/155, PDF p. 172); Note 45e(i)–(ii) (Daftar 5/197–5/202, PDF pp. 214–219); Note 45i (Daftar 5/208, PDF p. 225); Note 51 (Daftar 5/235, PDF p. 252). VERIFIED (PHE FS 2024, on disk; text dump, Indonesian and English columns read).</p></li><li><p>Lecture notes this series follows, "Inter 2 – Chapter 13 – Current Liabilities, Provisions and Contingencies" (Rumpun Akuntansi Keuangan): recognition criteria, best estimate, litigation, assurance and service warranties, consideration payable (mapped to PSAK 115), environmental provisions (the Pertamina drilling platform, USD 1,241,840 and the USD 100,000 settlement gain), onerous contracts, restructuring, contingent liabilities and assets. NOTES. The notes' warranty entry credits "warranty expense" instead of the liability (PDF p. 7), a slip, and they describe environmental liabilities in Kieso's fair value terms; corrected here to PSAK 237's best estimate. Expected value and discounting follow the standard.</p></li><li><p>PSAK 237 <em>Provisi, Liabilitas Kontinjensi, dan Aset Kontinjensi</em> (IAS 37), paras. 1–2, 5, 7, 10–35, 36–43, 45–55, 59–61, 63, 65–69, 72, 80–81, 84–86, 89; para. 5(f) on contingent consideration re-checked in the current consolidated text (faronline.se). VERIFIED (web, IFRS Foundation text of IAS 37 reproduced at ktkt.uel.edu.vn; that consolidation predates the 2020 amendment). The 2020 "Onerous Contracts — Cost of Fulfilling a Contract" amendment: VERIFIED in the Inventories essay (web, IFRS Foundation).</p></li><li><p>ISAK 101 (IFRIC 1 <em>Changes in Existing Decommissioning, Restoration and Similar Liabilities</em>): cost model, cap at carrying amount, impairment indicator, end of useful life, unwinding as finance cost and no capitalisation. VERIFIED (web, BDO New Zealand summary of NZ IFRIC 1, 2025). Paragraph numbers not cited.</p></li><li><p>IFRIC 5 <em>Rights to Interests arising from Decommissioning, Restoration and Environmental Rehabilitation Funds</em>: separate recognition and the lower-of measurement. VERIFIED (web, IAS Plus; BDO NZ summary). No ISAK 105 or other ISAK for IFRIC 5 in the list: VERIFIED (web, IAI, "Komparasi Perubahan Penomoran PSAK ISAK SAK Indonesia"; confirmed in the independent recheck). IFRIC 5.9 lower-of measurement: VERIFIED (web, secondary, BDO).</p></li><li><p>PSAK numbers PSAK 237 (57), PSAK 103 (22), PSAK 111 (66), PSAK 113 (68), PSAK 117 (74), ISAK 101 (ISAK 9), ISAK 121 (ISAK 30): VERIFIED (web, IAI renumbering list). PSAK 103 contingent consideration (paras. 39–40, 58), PSAK 111.21A (acquired interests in joint operations that are businesses), PSAK 216.16(c) and 18, PSAK 202, PSAK 109, PSAK 117, ISAK 121 content: STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED here, except as quoted in PHE's Note 3d.</p></li><li><p>Kieso, Weygandt and Warfield, <em>Intermediate Accounting</em>, IFRS edition, chapter on current liabilities, provisions and contingencies, the textbook the lecture notes follow. Not on disk; NOT VERIFIED.</p></li><li><p>Peraturan Menteri ESDM No. 15/2018 on post-operation activities in upstream oil and gas: ASR funds, joint account with SKK Migas, cost recovery and gross split treatment. VERIFIED (primary: Berita Negara 2018 No. 305, Arts. 11(2)(b), 11(3), 12(1)–(2), checked in the independent recheck; also SSEK, Ditjen Migas).</p></li><li><p>PP No. 78/2010 on reclamation and post-mining, with Permen ESDM 26/2018: reclamation and post-mining guarantees. VERIFIED (web, secondary: BPK regional notes, law-firm summaries). Current amendments not checked.</p></li></ol>$c$,
       content_json = $j${"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"In brief","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A provision is a liability of uncertain timing or amount. It is recognised when a past event has left a present obligation the entity cannot realistically avoid, an outflow is more likely than not, and the amount can be estimated. Anything weaker is a contingent liability, disclosed unless remote.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A provision is measured at the best estimate of what it would rationally cost to settle the obligation today. That is an expected value over a large population or the most likely outcome adjusted for skew for a single obligation, discounted at a pre-tax rate that matches the cash flows (nominal with nominal, real with real).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A decommissioning obligation is a provision whose cost is added to the asset that caused it. The discount unwinds as a finance cost, and changes in the estimate or the rate move the asset, not profit, until the asset is used up. PHE carries 3,826,222 of it, its largest liability.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"horizontalRule"},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PT Pertamina Hulu Energi and subsidiaries (PHE) report a provision for decommissioning and site restoration of 3,826,222 at 31 December 2024 against 3,784,917 a year earlier, in USD thousand (PHE FS 2024, statement of financial position, Daftar 1/2, PDF p. 12; \"Daftar\" is the printed page). It is the largest liability line, 25.0 percent of total liabilities and 12.6 percent of total assets (22.7 and 12.3 percent a year earlier). This page also covers other non-current liabilities, 281,228 (273,774 in 2023).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A student should come away able to tell a provision from a contingent liability, measure it, journal a decommissioning provision through its life, and see where other standards take over.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"All amounts are in USD thousand unless stated. Figures from PHE's statements are cited to their note. Every other number is labelled an illustration.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1. What a provision is","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A provision is a liability whose timing or amount is uncertain (PSAK 237.10; formerly PSAK 57, IAS 37). It is still a liability, a present obligation from a past event settled by an outflow of resources. The uncertainty sets it apart from other liabilities (para. 11).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Trade payables","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Accruals","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Provisions","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Goods or services","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"received","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"received","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"often none involved","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Amount and date","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"invoiced or formally agreed","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"estimated, with little uncertainty","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"uncertain in timing or amount","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PHE example, 2024","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"trade payables 1,091,822","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"accrued expenses 1,897,743","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"this line, 3,826,222","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Allowances against assets, such as PHE's \"provision for impairment of non-financial assets\", are not PSAK 237 provisions (para. 7). A provision is current or non-current by expected settlement date; PHE presents this one as wholly non-current.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2. Recognition","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A provision is recognised only when three conditions hold together (para. 14): a present obligation, legal or constructive, from a past event; a probable outflow of resources; and a reliable estimate.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Present obligation from a past event.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" The past event is the ","type":"text"},{"text":"obligating event","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", one that leaves the entity no realistic alternative to settling (para. 17). A ","type":"text"},{"text":"legal obligation","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" comes from contract or law; a ","type":"text"},{"text":"constructive obligation","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" from the entity's own policy or practice that has created a valid expectation in others (para. 10).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The test that does most of the work is whether the obligation exists independently of the entity's future actions (para. 19). The standard's own example is upstream. Decommissioning an oil installation is provided for to the extent damage has already been done, while a cost the entity could avoid by operating differently, such as fitting new filters, is not. For example (illustration), a subsidiary that has drilled 12 wells and plans 8 more provides for plugging 12; the 8 could still go undrilled.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The same logic rules out provisions for future operating losses (para. 63); an expected loss is an impairment indicator instead (para. 65).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Probable outflow.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Probable means more likely than not, a probability above 50 percent (para. 23). For many similar obligations, such as warranties, the test is applied to the class as a whole (para. 24).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Reliable estimate.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" The standard expects a range of outcomes to be estimable in all but extremely rare cases (para. 25). Where none can be made, the liability is disclosed as a contingent liability (para. 26).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"3. Measurement","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The amount recognised is the ","type":"text"},{"text":"best estimate","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" of the expenditure required to settle the obligation at the reporting date (para. 36). That is the amount the entity would rationally pay to settle it, or to transfer it to a third party, today (para. 37).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Expected value for a large population.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" When the provision covers many items, outcomes are weighted by their probabilities (para. 39). For example (illustration), a subsidiary must plug 40 old onshore wells. Experience says 70 percent need a simple cement plug at 400 each, 25 percent need remedial cementing at 700, and 5 percent have stuck pipe that must be fished out at 1,600.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Expected cost per well = 70% × 400 + 25% × 700 + 5% × 1,600 = 280 + 175 + 80 = 535","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Provision, before discounting = 40 × 535 = 21,400","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Most likely outcome for a single obligation.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" One platform has one outcome, so the starting point is the most likely one. Other outcomes still count. If they are mostly higher, the best estimate is higher (para. 40). For example (illustration), removing one platform will most likely cost 30,000 (60 percent), but there is a 30 percent chance of 45,000 if the jacket is corroded and a 10 percent chance of 25,000 if a heavy-lift vessel is already in the area.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Most likely outcome = 30,000; probability-weighted outcome = 60% × 30,000 + 30% × 45,000 + 10% × 25,000 = 34,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The skew is upward, so the best estimate lies above 30,000.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Risk.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Uncertainty is reflected once, in the cash flows or the rate, never both (paras. 42–43 and 47).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Present value.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Where timing matters, the provision is the present value of the expected outflows, at a pre-tax rate reflecting current market assessments of the time value of money and the risks specific to the liability (paras. 45–47). Cash flows and rate must match. Suppose the 34,000 is in today's prices, the work happens in 10 years, cost inflation is 2.5 percent and the nominal rate is 5.0 percent.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Nominal method: 34,000 × 1.025^10 = 43,523 in year-10 prices; 43,523 / 1.05^10 = 26,719","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Real method: real rate = 1.05 / 1.025 − 1 = 2.439%; 34,000 / 1.02439^10 = 26,719","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Mixed (today's prices at the nominal rate): 34,000 / 1.05^10 = 20,873, understated by 5,846","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Future events, disposals and reimbursements.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Expected technology savings count only on objective evidence, new legislation only when virtually certain (paras. 48–50), and scrap proceeds not at all (para. 51). A reimbursement, for example from an insurer, is a separate asset recognised only when virtually certain and capped at the provision (para. 53); only the expense may be shown net (para. 54).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"4. Common provisions","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The lecture notes list six forms. Litigation, warranties, onerous contracts and restructuring follow, environmental obligations get section 5, and consideration payable has left PSAK 237.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Litigation.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" A lawsuit needs a cause before the reporting date, a probable loss and an estimate; expert opinion is evidence (para. 16).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Warranties.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" An ","type":"text"},{"text":"assurance-type warranty","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" promises that the product works as specified, so it is a cost of the sale, provided for when the sale is made. A ","type":"text"},{"text":"service-type warranty","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" sells extra cover, a separate performance obligation under PSAK 115 (see the Deferred revenue essay). In the notes' example a seller sells 1,000 units at USD 500 each, expects warranty repairs on 100 units at USD 200 each (full amounts), pays 20 claims in the year and expects 80 next year.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Paid in the year: Dr Warranty expense 4,000 / Cr Cash, inventories 4,000","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"31 December: Dr Warranty expense 16,000 / Cr Warranty liability 16,000 (80 × 200)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Consideration payable","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" to customers, such as premiums and rebates, is now part of revenue under PSAK 115. It reduces the transaction price, and an expected refund is a refund liability (see the Deferred revenue essay).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Onerous contracts.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" A contract is ","type":"text"},{"text":"onerous","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" when the unavoidable cost of meeting it exceeds the benefit expected from it (para. 10), and the unavoidable cost is the ","type":"text"},{"text":"least net cost of exiting","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", the lower of the cost of fulfilling it net of what fulfilling still earns and the penalty for walking away (para. 68). Assets dedicated to the contract are tested for impairment first (para. 69). Since the 2020 amendment the cost of fulfilling includes both incremental costs and an allocation of other directly related costs. For example (illustration), a subsidiary has a drilling services contract costing 60,000 for 2025, and the programme is cancelled. Exiting costs a penalty of 25,000; re-selling the services to another operator would bring in 40,000.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cost of fulfilling, net of benefits = 60,000 − 40,000 = 20,000; penalty = 25,000","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Provision = lower of the two = 20,000","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Loss on onerous contract 20,000 / Cr Provision for onerous contract 20,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Restructuring.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" A constructive obligation needs a detailed formal plan, started or announced to those affected (para. 72). Only direct costs not associated with continuing activities are included, so retraining staff who stay, marketing and new systems are excluded (paras. 80–81).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"5. Decommissioning: the asset retirement obligation","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Installing a well, platform or pipeline creates an obligation to plug, remove and restore the site at the end of field life. PHE recognises it for \"oil and gas production facilities, wells, pipelines and related assets in accordance with the provisions in the PSC or in line with applicable regulations\" (Note 3o, Daftar 5/60, PDF p. 77). The Oil and gas properties essay shows the asset side; this section follows the liability.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Initial recognition.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" The provision is recognised on installation and grows as more wells are drilled. The cost goes into the asset under PSAK 216.16(c) (formerly PSAK 16, IAS 16), because removing the platform is part of what it costs to use it. The removal cost has no future benefit of its own, so it is not a separate asset (the lecture notes, following Kieso). Where the obligation arises from producing inventory during a period, as when a mine disturbs more land to extract this year's ore, the cost goes to inventory under PSAK 202 instead.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"For example (illustration), a PHE subsidiary completes an offshore platform with 20 wells on 31 December 2024. Removal and plugging are expected at the end of 2036, 12 years later. In today's prices the engineers see a 60 percent chance of 80,000, 30 percent of 100,000 and 10 percent of 140,000. Cost inflation is 2.5 percent and the pre-tax nominal discount rate 5.0 percent.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Expected cost in today's prices = 60% × 80,000 + 30% × 100,000 + 10% × 140,000 = 92,000","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"In 2036 prices = 92,000 × 1.025^12 = 123,730","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Present value at 31 December 2024 = 123,730 / 1.05^12 = 68,897","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"One platform is a single obligation, so 80,000 is the most likely outcome. The other outcomes are all higher, so the best estimate lies above it, and this page uses the weighted 92,000, as section 3 allowed.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Oil and gas properties 68,897 / Cr Provision for decommissioning 68,897","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Unwinding of the discount.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" As settlement approaches, the present value rises by the discount rate. The increase is a ","type":"text"},{"text":"finance cost","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (para. 60) that ISAK 101 (formerly ISAK 9, IFRIC 1) sends to profit or loss and forbids capitalising. PHE calls it accretion.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2025: 68,897 × 5% = 3,445; provision = 72,342","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2026: 72,342 × 5% = 3,617; provision = 75,959","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Finance cost 3,445 / Cr Provision for decommissioning 3,445 (2026: 3,617)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Changes in estimate.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" The expected cash flows, their timing and the discount rate all move the provision. Under ISAK 101, for an asset carried at cost, the change is added to or deducted from the asset and depleted over its remaining life. A deduction may not exceed the asset's carrying amount, and any excess goes to profit. An addition is considered as a possible impairment indicator and, if it is one, the asset is tested. Once the asset reaches the end of its useful life, every later change goes to profit or loss. PHE's policy states the add/deduct rule, the cap and the impairment check (Note 3o).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Suppose that at the end of 2026 the market rate for this liability falls to 4.0 percent, with 10 years to go and the cash estimate unchanged.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Remeasured provision = 123,730 / 1.04^10 = 83,588; increase = 83,588 − 75,959 = 7,629","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Oil and gas properties 7,629 / Cr Provision for decommissioning 7,629","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"From 2027 the provision unwinds at 4.0 percent. Now suppose that at the end of 2033, with three years left, a cheaper plugging method cuts the 2036 estimate by 12,000, and the platform's carrying amount is down to 6,000.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Provision before the change = 123,730 / 1.04^3 = 109,996","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Decrease = 12,000 / 1.04^3 = 10,668; new provision = 99,328","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Provision for decommissioning 10,668 / Cr Oil and gas properties 6,000 / Cr Gain on decommissioning estimate 4,668","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Settlement.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" The provision is used only for its original purpose (para. 61). By the end of 2036 the provision has unwound to the revised nominal estimate of 123,730 − 12,000 = 111,730. If the contractor charges 115,000, the 3,270 difference is a loss of that period.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Provision for decommissioning 111,730, Dr Loss on settlement 3,270 / Cr Cash 115,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Sensitivity.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" A long-dated provision moves sharply with its discount rate. Take PHE's 2024 balance as the starting point, and treat it, for illustration only, as one payment 15 years away at 3.17 percent, the ratio of PHE's 2024 accretion to its opening balance (Note 29).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Implied payment in 15 years = 3,826,222 × 1.0317^15 = 6,110,428","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"At 4.17%: 6,110,428 / 1.0417^15 = 3,310,790, a fall of 515,432 (13.5%)","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"At 2.17%: 6,110,428 / 1.0217^15 = 4,428,134, a rise of 601,912 (15.7%)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The rise exceeds the fall because present value is convex in the rate. Under ISAK 101 most of either swing would land on oil and gas properties, not profit.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"6. Contingent liabilities and contingent assets","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A ","type":"text"},{"text":"contingent liability","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" is either a possible obligation whose existence will be confirmed only by uncertain future events outside the entity's control, or a present obligation that fails the probability or estimate test (para. 10). It is never recognised (para. 27). It is disclosed, with an estimate of its effect where practicable, unless remote (paras. 28 and 86), and becomes a provision in the period the outflow turns probable (para. 30).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Joint and several liability.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" The part of a joint obligation expected to be met by the others is a contingent liability, and the entity provides only for its own probable share (para. 29). For example (illustration), a PHE subsidiary holds 30 percent of a block whose partners are jointly and severally liable for a 10,000 remediation order.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Provision = 30% × 10,000 = 3,000; contingent liability disclosed = 7,000, unless the partners look unable to pay","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PHE discloses a real case. A 2022 ICC award orders Pertamina E&P Libya and Pertamina, jointly and severally, to pay Libya's national oil company 24,000 and 5,200 plus interest for unfulfilled exploration commitments (Note 45e(i), Daftar 5/197–5/198, PDF pp. 214–215).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PHE is also guarantor for a former Australian subsidiary's share of abandonment costs in the Basker Manta Gummy block. The operator sued in Victoria; PHE's position, on legal advice, is that it owes nothing because abandonment was not completed within five years of withdrawal (Note 45e(ii), Daftar 5/198–5/202, PDF pp. 215–219). The note discloses no amount and does not say whether a provision is held. Its place under Note 45, \"commitments and contingencies\", with no matching provision in Notes 28 or 29, is consistent with a contingent liability.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A ","type":"text"},{"text":"contingent asset","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" is a possible asset from past events, such as a claim being pursued in court (paras. 10 and 32). It is not recognised (paras. 31 and 33), is disclosed when an inflow is probable (para. 34), and is recognised only when virtually certain (para. 35). A liability is booked at more likely than not, an asset only at virtually certain. For example (illustration), an insurance claim of 5,000 disputed at year end is disclosed; when the insurer accepts it in writing next year, it is booked.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Insurance claim receivable 5,000 / Cr Other income 5,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"7. Decommissioning funds and the other non-current liabilities","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Funds set aside.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Under IFRIC 5 a contributor to a decommissioning fund keeps its obligation as a liability and recognises its interest in the fund separately; without control or influence over the fund, the reimbursement right is measured at the lower of the provision and its share of the fair value of the fund's net assets. IAI's renumbering list has no ISAK for IFRIC 5, but PSAK 237.53 and the offsetting ban in PSAK 201 (formerly PSAK 1, IAS 1) give the same gross answer. For example (illustration), a provision of 10,000 and a fund of 6,000 appear as a liability of 10,000 and an asset of 6,000, never a net liability of 4,000.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PHE's funds sit in joint accounts with SKK Migas, usable only for restoration with its approval, and are presented as restricted cash (Note 3o, Daftar 5/61, PDF p. 78), 1,890,493 non-current at the end of 2024 (Note 7, Daftar 5/93–5/95, PDF pp. 110–112), plus its share of joint-operation funds, 66,719, in other non-current assets (Note 14b, Daftar 5/105, PDF p. 122). Their fall from 296,555 is mostly a transfer into PHR's joint account (p. 123).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Other non-current liabilities.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" PHE's Note 28 shows why a liability with \"contingent\" in its name need not be a PSAK 237 item (Daftar 5/144, PDF p. 161).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2024","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2023","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Governing standard","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Acquisition of the Masela block","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"167,902","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"159,311","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 103, contingent consideration","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Penalty for unfulfilled work commitments (Libya)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"39,600","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"39,600","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"liability; possibly the ICC award in Note 45e(i), not stated","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Monitoring and recovery of two wells","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"25,948","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"25,959","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 237, decommissioning-type","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Utilisation of the Badak LNG plant","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"18,313","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"22,503","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"obligation under PHI's WBX-NR sales contract; standard not stated","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSC partner","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"10,387","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"9,100","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"not explained","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Others","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"19,078","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"17,312","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"includes Simenggaris sunk cost 9,720 and abandonment costs 7,530","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Total","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"281,228","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"273,774","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PHE Masela bought a 20 percent participating interest from Shell, completed on 18 October 2023 (Note 5f, Daftar 5/88, PDF p. 105), and owes a further payment if the block's partners take a final investment decision (FID). Under PSAK 103 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Business Combinations","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (formerly PSAK 22, IFRS 3) contingent consideration is part of the price, recognised at fair value at the acquisition date (PHE measures it at present value under PSAK 113, Note 28) and, if it is a liability, remeasured at fair value through profit or loss (Note 3d, Daftar 5/36, PDF p. 53). PHE applies PSAK 103 to this acquired interest in a joint operation, consistent with PSAK 111's rule for interests that are businesses. PSAK 237 does not apply, both because another standard covers it (para. 5) and because a contractual obligation to pay cash is a financial liability (para. 2).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The two standards treat the same odds differently. For example (illustration), an acquirer will pay 100,000 if FID is taken in four years, judged 40 percent likely, with a discount rate of 8 percent.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Under PSAK 237 logic: outflow 40% < 50%, nothing recognised, contingent liability disclosed","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Under PSAK 103: fair value = 40% × 100,000 / 1.08^4 = 29,401, recognised as part of the cost of the acquisition","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A year later the odds rise to 60%: 60% × 100,000 / 1.08^3 = 47,630; Dr Loss on remeasurement 18,229 / Cr Contingent consideration liability 18,229","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"8. Provisions across industries","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Mining.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Indonesian miners provide for reclamation and post-mining closure under PP 78/2010 and also place guarantees, such as time deposits, that do not discharge the obligation, so both are shown gross. Power plants carry the same provision. Mining also tests section 5's asset-or-inventory split every year. For example (illustration), a coal pit incurs a restoration obligation of 2,000 for land disturbed to extract this year's coal and 3,000 for a haul road built to serve the mine for ten years.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Inventories, coal 2,000 / Cr Provision for reclamation 2,000","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Mine properties 3,000 / Cr Provision for reclamation 3,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Lessees and telecom towers.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Restoring leased land puts the cost into the right-of-use asset and the obligation into a PSAK 237 provision (see the Right-of-use essay).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Levies.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" ISAK 121 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Levies","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (formerly ISAK 30, IFRIC 21) makes the obligating event the activity that triggers payment under the law, not the period the levy relates to. For example (illustration), a levy of 1,200 for a year that is triggered by being in operation on 1 January is a full liability of 1,200 on 1 January, not 100 a month.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Banks and insurers.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Financial guarantees and loan commitments fall under PSAK 109 (formerly PSAK 71, IFRS 9), with expected credit losses, not under PSAK 237 (para. 2). Insurers' claims liabilities fall under PSAK 117 (formerly PSAK 74, IFRS 17).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"9. Presentation and analysis: PHE's Note 29","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 237 asks for a reconciliation showing additions, amounts used, unused amounts reversed and the unwinding (para. 84), and for the expected timing, uncertainties, major assumptions and reimbursements (para. 85). PHE gives a three-line movement (Note 29, Daftar 5/145, PDF p. 162).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2024","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2023","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Balance at beginning of year","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"3,784,917","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"3,460,330","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Additions/(deductions) and revisions of estimates","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"78,620","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"211,366","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Accretion expense","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"119,925","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"113,221","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Balance at end of year","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"3,826,222","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"3,784,917","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2023: 3,460,330 + 211,366 + 113,221 = 3,784,917","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2024: 3,784,917 + 78,620 + 119,925 = 3,983,462, not 3,826,222; 3,784,917 − 78,620 + 119,925 = 3,826,222","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The 2024 revisions only foot as a deduction of 78,620, though they are printed without brackets. Alternatively PHE omitted a line: if 78,620 is an addition, an unprinted reduction of 157,240 is needed. The Oil and gas properties essay gives the same two readings. Three more points stand out.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"There is no line for amounts used. Either no abandonment spending was charged in two years, or it is netted inside the revisions, though paragraph 84 asks for it separately.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Note 51 reports non-cash decommissioning adjustments to oil and gas properties of +12,627 in 2024 and 202,685 in 2023 (Daftar 5/235, PDF p. 252), against Note 29 revisions of 78,620 (a deduction on the footing reading) and 211,366. The gaps of 91,247 and 8,681 could be utilisation, revisions taken to profit, disposals or exchange differences; the FS do not say.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The discount rate, inflation assumption and timing are not disclosed. Note 29 describes ARO as the present value of costs \"expected to be incurred during the terms of the PSCs\" and Note 4b(ii) lists the main uncertainties (Daftar 5/80, PDF p. 97).","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Accretion / opening provision = 119,925 / 3,784,917 = 3.17% (2023: 113,221 / 3,460,330 = 3.27%)","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Accretion / finance cost = 119,925 / 453,906 = 26.4% (Note 41, Daftar 5/150, PDF p. 167)","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Funds held / provision = (1,890,493 + 66,719) / 3,826,222 = 51.2% (2023: 52.8%)","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Deferred tax on the provision = 66,352 + 412,586 = 478,938, or 12.5% of the provision (Note 43e, Daftar 5/155, PDF p. 172)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"About half the obligation, at present value, is backed by cash usable for nothing else. The deferred tax ratio is far below PHE's tax rates, plausibly because ASR deposits are recoverable or deductible when placed (section 10), leaving much of the provision with no future deduction; the mechanics belong to the income tax essays. Note 28's 2023 lines also add to 273,785, not the 273,774 printed, a difference of 11.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"10. Upstream note: abandonment and site restoration under the PSC","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Indonesian upstream contractors set aside abandonment and site restoration (ASR) funds under Minister of Energy and Mineral Resources Regulation 15/2018, starting in the year production becomes commercial, in a joint account with SKK Migas at a state-owned bank. Under cost recovery the amounts set aside are recoverable costs; under gross split they are deductible for income tax. PHE OSES, for example, deposited funds for both halves of 2024 on SKK Migas instruction (Note 45i, Daftar 5/208, PDF p. 225).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"11. Related lines","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"oil-gas-properties-net","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/oil-gas-properties-net","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": the asset that absorbs the initial estimate and later revisions.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"restricted-cash-non-current","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/restricted-cash","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": the ASR funds.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"other-non-current-assets","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/advances-prepayments-current","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": funds in joint operations.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"finance-cost","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/finance-cost","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": the accretion.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"deferred-tax-liabilities","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/deferred-tax-assets","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": the deductible temporary difference on the provision.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"trade-payables","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/trade-payables","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"text":"accrued-expenses","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/accrued-expenses","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": the liabilities with certain or nearly certain amounts.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"other-non-current-liabilities","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"text":": covered here, section 7.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Least-confident assertions","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"That PHE treats the Basker Manta Gummy claim as a contingent liability is inferred from its place in Note 45 (commitments and contingencies) and the absence of an amount or provision. The item itself is not labelled.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Reading Note 29's 78,620 as a deduction rests on footing alone; PHE may instead have omitted a line of 157,240. The reasons for the gaps to Note 51 are not disclosed, and the listed causes are possibilities, not findings. That ASR deposits explain the low deferred tax ratio is this page's reading.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The 3.17 percent \"rate\" and the single 15-year payment in the sensitivity are illustrative simplifications. PHE discloses neither its discount rate nor the timing, and a portfolio of fields with different rates and dates would respond differently.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"That Indonesia has no ISAK equivalent of IFRIC 5 rests on IAI's renumbering list, which lists only interpretations in force. Whether PHE's joint ASR accounts would fall within IFRIC 5's scope if it applied is this page's reading; PHE presents them as restricted cash.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The governing standards given for the Note 28 items are this page's reading of one-line descriptions. The Libya penalty may be the ICC award of Note 45e(i); the FS do not link them, and the balance shows no interest accrual. The 51.2 percent funding ratio assumes all non-current restricted cash is ASR funds, as Note 7's description implies but does not state line by line.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"References","type":"text"}]},{"type":"orderedList","attrs":{"type":null,"start":1},"content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PT Pertamina Hulu Energi dan Entitas Anak, consolidated financial statements 31 December 2024 (audited, USD thousand): statement of financial position (Daftar 1/2, PDF p. 12); Note 3d (Daftar 5/36, PDF p. 53); Note 3o (Daftar 5/60–5/61, PDF pp. 77–78); Note 4b(ii) (Daftar 5/80, PDF p. 97); Note 5f (Daftar 5/88, PDF p. 105); Note 7 (Daftar 5/93–5/95, PDF pp. 110–112); Note 14b (Daftar 5/105–5/106, PDF pp. 122–123); Note 28 (Daftar 5/144, PDF p. 161); Note 29 (Daftar 5/145, PDF p. 162); Note 41 (Daftar 5/150, PDF p. 167); Note 43e (Daftar 5/155, PDF p. 172); Note 45e(i)–(ii) (Daftar 5/197–5/202, PDF pp. 214–219); Note 45i (Daftar 5/208, PDF p. 225); Note 51 (Daftar 5/235, PDF p. 252). VERIFIED (PHE FS 2024, on disk; text dump, Indonesian and English columns read).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Lecture notes this series follows, \"Inter 2 – Chapter 13 – Current Liabilities, Provisions and Contingencies\" (Rumpun Akuntansi Keuangan): recognition criteria, best estimate, litigation, assurance and service warranties, consideration payable (mapped to PSAK 115), environmental provisions (the Pertamina drilling platform, USD 1,241,840 and the USD 100,000 settlement gain), onerous contracts, restructuring, contingent liabilities and assets. NOTES. The notes' warranty entry credits \"warranty expense\" instead of the liability (PDF p. 7), a slip, and they describe environmental liabilities in Kieso's fair value terms; corrected here to PSAK 237's best estimate. Expected value and discounting follow the standard.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 237 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Provisi, Liabilitas Kontinjensi, dan Aset Kontinjensi","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (IAS 37), paras. 1–2, 5, 7, 10–35, 36–43, 45–55, 59–61, 63, 65–69, 72, 80–81, 84–86, 89; para. 5(f) on contingent consideration re-checked in the current consolidated text (faronline.se). VERIFIED (web, IFRS Foundation text of IAS 37 reproduced at ktkt.uel.edu.vn; that consolidation predates the 2020 amendment). The 2020 \"Onerous Contracts — Cost of Fulfilling a Contract\" amendment: VERIFIED in the Inventories essay (web, IFRS Foundation).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"ISAK 101 (IFRIC 1 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Changes in Existing Decommissioning, Restoration and Similar Liabilities","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":"): cost model, cap at carrying amount, impairment indicator, end of useful life, unwinding as finance cost and no capitalisation. VERIFIED (web, BDO New Zealand summary of NZ IFRIC 1, 2025). Paragraph numbers not cited.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"IFRIC 5 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Rights to Interests arising from Decommissioning, Restoration and Environmental Rehabilitation Funds","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":": separate recognition and the lower-of measurement. VERIFIED (web, IAS Plus; BDO NZ summary). No ISAK 105 or other ISAK for IFRIC 5 in the list: VERIFIED (web, IAI, \"Komparasi Perubahan Penomoran PSAK ISAK SAK Indonesia\"; confirmed in the independent recheck). IFRIC 5.9 lower-of measurement: VERIFIED (web, secondary, BDO).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK numbers PSAK 237 (57), PSAK 103 (22), PSAK 111 (66), PSAK 113 (68), PSAK 117 (74), ISAK 101 (ISAK 9), ISAK 121 (ISAK 30): VERIFIED (web, IAI renumbering list). PSAK 103 contingent consideration (paras. 39–40, 58), PSAK 111.21A (acquired interests in joint operations that are businesses), PSAK 216.16(c) and 18, PSAK 202, PSAK 109, PSAK 117, ISAK 121 content: STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED here, except as quoted in PHE's Note 3d.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Kieso, Weygandt and Warfield, ","type":"text"},{"text":"Intermediate Accounting","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", IFRS edition, chapter on current liabilities, provisions and contingencies, the textbook the lecture notes follow. Not on disk; NOT VERIFIED.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Peraturan Menteri ESDM No. 15/2018 on post-operation activities in upstream oil and gas: ASR funds, joint account with SKK Migas, cost recovery and gross split treatment. VERIFIED (primary: Berita Negara 2018 No. 305, Arts. 11(2)(b), 11(3), 12(1)–(2), checked in the independent recheck; also SSEK, Ditjen Migas).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PP No. 78/2010 on reclamation and post-mining, with Permen ESDM 26/2018: reclamation and post-mining guarantees. VERIFIED (web, secondary: BPK regional notes, law-firm summaries). Current amendments not checked.","type":"text"}]}]}]}]}$j$::jsonb,
       presentation = $p${"deck":"The theory of provisions and contingencies from recognition to disclosure, with asset retirement obligations as the main application, worked through PHE's largest liability and set beside the industries with their own rules","genre":null,"author_bio":null,"references":[],"hero_caption":null,"key_takeaways":["A provision is a liability of uncertain timing or amount. It is recognised when a past event has left a present obligation the entity cannot realistically avoid, an outflow is more likely than not, and the amount can be estimated. Anything weaker is a contingent liability, disclosed unless remote.","A provision is measured at the best estimate of what it would rationally cost to settle the obligation today. That is an expected value over a large population or the most likely outcome adjusted for skew for a single obligation, discounted at a pre-tax rate that matches the cash flows (nominal with nominal, real with real).","A decommissioning obligation is a provision whose cost is added to the asset that caused it. The discount unwinds as a finance cost, and changes in the estimate or the rate move the asset, not profit, until the asset is used up. PHE carries 3,826,222 of it, its largest liability."],"revision_note":null}$p$::jsonb,
       updated_at = now()
 WHERE id = '8e26845c-467c-4c83-b593-a0c647e354be';
INSERT INTO public.essay_revisions (essay_id, revision_no, change_type, title, snippet, content_json, status, change_summary, changed_by)
SELECT e.id, coalesce((SELECT max(x.revision_no) FROM public.essay_revisions x WHERE x.essay_id = e.id), 0) + 1, 'rollback',
       e.title, e.snippet, e.content_json, e.status, $s$Restored from the snapshot taken before the lecture-notes removal$s$, null
  FROM public.essays e WHERE e.id = '8e26845c-467c-4c83-b593-a0c647e354be';

-- deferred-revenue-contract-liabilities-take-or-pay
UPDATE public.essays
   SET content = $c$<p><strong>In brief</strong></p><ul><li><p>Deferred revenue is a contract liability, an obligation to transfer goods or services for which the customer has already paid or for which payment is already due. It is recognised at the earlier of the two and released to revenue as each performance obligation is satisfied.</p></li><li><p>The amount in the liability depends on the five steps of PSAK 115. Allocation decides how much of a payment belongs to undelivered obligations, a significant financing component can make the liability grow with interest, and customer options and service warranties create obligations that must be deferred even when nothing was prepaid for them.</p></li><li><p>Rights that customers pay for but never use, such as unused make-up gas under a take-or-pay contract, are breakage. They become revenue in proportion to the rights customers do use or, when the seller does not expect to be entitled to breakage, only once use becomes remote.</p></li></ul><hr><p>PT Pertamina Hulu Energi and subsidiaries (PHE) report deferred revenue, current maturities, of 6,012 at 31 December 2024 against 4,126 a year earlier, with a non-current portion of 13,118 against 7,017, in USD thousand (PHE FS 2024, statement of financial position, Daftar 1/2, PDF p. 12; "Daftar" is the printed page). The current line is 0.10 percent of current liabilities (0.07 percent a year earlier), and the two lines together, 19,130, are 0.12 percent of total liabilities (0.07 percent a year earlier) and 0.06 percent of total assets.</p><p>After reading, a student should be able to say when a customer payment becomes a liability, measure it through the five steps, release it with journals, and recognise the same mechanics in airlines, telecoms and property developers.</p><p>All amounts are in USD thousand unless stated. Figures from PHE's statements are cited to their note. Every other number is labelled an illustration. The governing standard is PSAK 115 <em>Pendapatan dari Kontrak dengan Pelanggan</em> (formerly PSAK 72, IFRS 15), and PHE's notes use the new number.</p><h2>1. What deferred revenue is</h2><p>Cash received before performance is owed back in goods or services, so it is a liability. Kieso's older vocabulary calls it <em>unearned revenue</em> and lists it among current liabilities. The lecture note for Ch 13 records it the classic way: 10,000 received on 2 December for goods shipped FOB destination is credited to unearned revenue, and moves to sales revenue when the goods arrive on 5 December. The same note keeps <em>customer advances and deposits</em> apart. A returnable deposit is owed back in cash, while unearned revenue is owed in goods or services, a line that returns in section 8.</p><p>PSAK 115 keeps the idea but rebuilds the reasoning. Its <em>asset-liability approach</em> recognises revenue when a contract asset rises or a contract liability falls, rather than when revenue is "earned and realised". Three balances follow.</p><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Balance</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>What it is</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>When it arises</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Receivable</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>An unconditional right to consideration: only the passage of time is needed before payment is due (para. 108)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Performance done, or payment due under a non-cancellable contract</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Contract asset</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>A right to consideration conditional on something other than time, usually further performance (para. 107)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Performance done ahead of the right to bill</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Contract liability</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>An obligation to transfer goods or services for which consideration has been received or is due (para. 106)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Payment received or due ahead of performance</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>The Trade receivables essay teaches the first two rows; this essay is about the third.</p><p>The contract liability is recognised "when the payment is made or the payment is due (whichever is earlier)" (para. 106). A payment that is due but unpaid creates the liability too, if the contract cannot be cancelled. For example (illustration), a PHE drilling subsidiary signs a non-cancellable contract on 1 October 2024 to drill one well and then monitor it for twelve months, for 3,000, invoiced on 1 November and payable on 1 December. On 1 December nothing has been paid and no drilling has started.</p><blockquote><p>Non-cancellable, payment due: Dr Trade receivables 3,000 / Cr Contract liability 3,000</p><p>Cancellable until drilling starts: no entry, because the right to payment is not yet unconditional</p></blockquote><p>Each contract is presented as one net position, a contract asset or a contract liability, never both (para. 105). PSAK 115 allows other names as long as receivables stay distinguishable (para. 109), which is why PHE and most Indonesian issuers still say <em>pendapatan tangguhan</em>, deferred revenue.</p><h2>2. The five steps and where the liability comes from</h2><p>Every contract liability is a product of the five steps that open the lecture note for Inter 2 Ch 18.</p><ol><li><p>Identify the contract with the customer.</p></li><li><p>Identify the <em>performance obligations</em>, the promises to transfer a distinct good or service.</p></li><li><p>Determine the <em>transaction price</em>, the consideration the entity expects to be entitled to.</p></li><li><p>Allocate the price to the obligations in proportion to their <em>standalone selling prices</em> (SSP), the prices at which each would sell on its own.</p></li><li><p>Recognise revenue when, or as, each obligation is satisfied by transferring control.</p></li></ol><p>The contract liability is the part of consideration received or due that step 4 has assigned to obligations step 5 has not yet satisfied. In the drilling contract, drilling and monitoring are distinct, since a customer could buy either from another contractor. Suppose drilling alone would sell for 2,700 and a year of monitoring for 900 (illustration).</p><blockquote><p>Total SSP = 2,700 + 900 = 3,600</p><p>Drilling = 3,000 × 2,700 / 3,600 = 2,250</p><p>Monitoring = 3,000 × 900 / 3,600 = 750</p></blockquote><p>The 600 bundle discount is spread across both obligations in proportion, unless there is evidence it belongs to one of them. The customer pays the 3,000 on 5 December. Drilling runs through December on the customer's well, so the customer controls the well as it is drilled and the obligation is satisfied over time; by 31 December the well is finished.</p><blockquote><p>5 December: Dr Cash 3,000 / Cr Trade receivables 3,000</p><p>31 December: Dr Contract liability 2,250 / Cr Revenue, drilling services 2,250</p></blockquote><p>The liability left at the year end is 750, all current because monitoring runs through 2025. Had the price been allocated by invoice line instead of SSP, with the contract billing 2,800 for drilling and 200 for monitoring, the year-end liability would be 200 and 550 of revenue would have been pulled into 2024. Step 4 exists to stop that.</p><h2>3. The transaction price: financing, refunds and rebates</h2><p>The transaction price is not always the cash received, and the differences flow into the liability.</p><p><strong>Significant financing component.</strong> When payment and performance are far apart, part of the payment is interest. If the customer pays early, the seller is borrowing from the customer, so the seller accretes interest on the contract liability and recognises more revenue than the cash received (paras. 60–65). The rate is the one a separate financing transaction between the two parties would carry, reflecting the credit of whoever receives the financing, here the seller (para. 64). For example (illustration), an industrial buyer prepays 10,000 on 1 January 2024 for gas a PHE subsidiary will deliver in full at the end of 2025, and the subsidiary could borrow for two years at 6 percent.</p><blockquote><p>Year 1 interest = 10,000 × 6% = 600; liability = 10,600</p><p>Year 2 interest = 10,600 × 6% = 636; liability = 11,236</p></blockquote><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Financing component recognised</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>No financing adjustment (if para. 62(a) or 63 applied)</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Receipt</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Cash 10,000 / Cr Contract liability 10,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Cash 10,000 / Cr Contract liability 10,000</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2024</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Interest expense 600 / Cr Contract liability 600</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>no entry</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2025</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Interest expense 636 / Cr Contract liability 636</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>no entry</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Delivery</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Contract liability 11,236 / Cr Revenue 11,236</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Contract liability 10,000 / Cr Revenue 10,000</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Revenue less finance cost is 10,000 either way, but the left column shows 11,236 of revenue and 1,236 of finance cost, which is what a buyer paying the cash price at delivery would produce plus a separate loan. In this fact pattern, with a fixed delivery date two years out, neither exception applies, but they cover many common cases. No adjustment is needed when, at inception, the gap between payment and transfer is expected to be one year or less (para. 63), and there is no significant financing component when the customer prepaid and itself decides when the goods are transferred (para. 62(a)). The second exception is the one that applies to take-or-pay make-up gas in section 5.</p><p><strong>Refund liabilities.</strong> Consideration the seller expects to give back in cash is not transaction price at all. It is a <em>refund liability</em> (para. 55), a separate liability settled in cash rather than in goods. For example (illustration), a buyer pays 5,000 for gas and will receive a 2 percent volume rebate in cash, which the seller expects it to earn.</p><blockquote><p>Dr Cash 5,000 / Cr Revenue 4,900 / Cr Refund liability 100</p></blockquote><p>Only an obligation to deliver goods or services is a contract liability. The distinction comes back in section 8, because PHE's classification blurs it.</p><h2>4. Releasing the liability: point in time or over time</h2><p>The liability falls when control passes, and control passes in one of two ways. An obligation is satisfied <em>over time</em> if the customer receives and consumes the benefit as the entity performs, if the customer controls the asset as it is created, or if the asset has no alternative use to the seller and the seller has an enforceable right to payment for work done to date (para. 35). Otherwise it is satisfied <em>at a point in time</em>, judged by indicators such as a present right to payment, legal title, physical possession, transfer of risks and rewards, and the customer's acceptance (para. 38).</p><p>For over-time obligations the entity picks a <em>measure of progress</em>. <em>Output methods</em> count what has been delivered (months of service, metres drilled, BBTU of gas; one BBTU is a billion British thermal units). <em>Input methods</em> count effort, such as cost incurred against total expected cost, Kieso's cost-to-cost method. Monitoring is a stand-ready service delivered evenly, so time elapsed is the natural output measure.</p><blockquote><p>Monthly release = 750 / 12 = 62.5</p><p>Each month of 2025: Dr Contract liability 62.5 / Cr Revenue, monitoring services 62.5</p></blockquote><p>Gas delivered through a pipeline meter is released quantity by quantity. Whether that is a series of point-in-time sales or one obligation satisfied over time depends on whether the buyer consumes the gas as it arrives (IASB/FASB Transition Resource Group, July 2015). PHE's policy treats goods as generally transferred at a point in time and recognises crude and gas revenue "based on the actual lifting amount and sold to customers" (Note 3p, Daftar 5/62, PDF p. 79). Either way a prepayment for gas is released as the gas is delivered.</p><h2>5. Take-or-pay and breakage</h2><p>A take-or-pay clause makes the buyer pay for a minimum quantity whether or not it takes it. Such contracts typically soften it with a <em>make-up right</em>: the buyer may take the gas it paid for but did not take in later years, usually only after taking that year's own minimum. PHE's policy describes this balance: deferred revenue is "amounts billed and collected involving 'take or pay' gas quantities, which will be recognized as revenue when the related gas quantity are delivered to customers or when the contract expires" (Note 3u, Daftar 5/71, PDF p. 88).</p><p>Take a contract of the kind behind PHE's line (illustration). A subsidiary sells gas at USD 6 per MMBtu, which is 6 per BBTU in USD thousand. The annual contract quantity is 2,000 BBTU and the take-or-pay level is 80 percent, so 1,600 BBTU. Make-up gas may be taken in the two years after the shortfall.</p><blockquote><p>Year 1: buyer takes 1,200 BBTU; revenue = 1,200 × 6 = 7,200</p><p>Shortfall = 1,600 − 1,200 = 400 BBTU, billed and paid = 400 × 6 = 2,400</p><p>Dr Cash 2,400 / Cr Contract liability, take-or-pay 2,400</p></blockquote><p>The 2,400 is not revenue in year 1. The buyer has paid for gas it can still demand, so the seller owes 400 BBTU. The buyer decides when to take it, so para. 62(a) removes any financing component. Without a make-up right the shortfall payment would be revenue in year 1, because the seller would owe nothing more for it; the make-up right is what creates the liability.</p><blockquote><p>Year 2: buyer takes 1,750 BBTU; 1,600 is year 2's own minimum, paid in cash (9,600); 150 BBTU is make-up</p><p>Dr Contract liability 900 / Cr Revenue 900 (150 × 6)</p><p>Year 3: buyer takes exactly 1,600 BBTU; 250 BBTU of make-up remains unused when the right expires</p></blockquote><p>The unused 250 BBTU is <em>breakage</em>: a right the customer paid for and never exercised. PSAK 115 gives two treatments (paras. B44–B47). If the seller expects to be entitled to breakage, it recognises the expected amount in proportion to the pattern of rights the customer exercises. If it does not, it waits until the likelihood of the customer exercising its remaining rights becomes remote. The expected amount is subject to the constraint on variable consideration, so the seller must be confident it will not reverse. Suppose the seller's history shows that 40 percent of make-up gas is never taken (illustration).</p><blockquote><p>Expected make-up taken = 400 × 60% = 240 BBTU; expected breakage = 160 × 6 = 960</p><p>Year 2 share of expected use = 150 / 240 = 62.5%; breakage recognised = 960 × 62.5% = 600</p><p>Year 3 remainder at expiry = 2,400 − 900 − 600 = 900</p></blockquote><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Revenue from the 2,400</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Remote approach (PHE's policy)</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Proportional approach</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Year 2</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>900</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>900 + 600 = 1,500</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Year 3, at expiry</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>1,500</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>900</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>Total</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>2,400</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>2,400</strong></p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Both release the same 2,400. The proportional approach brings breakage forward when the seller has evidence for it; the remote approach waits. PHE's wording, recognition "when the contract expires", fits the remote approach at its latest point and is not the proportional approach.</p><p>The current and non-current split follows the same schedule. A contract liability is current to the extent the seller expects to satisfy it within twelve months (PSAK 201.69, formerly PSAK 1, IAS 1), and it is settled by delivery or expiry, not cash. At the end of year 1, under the remote approach, the seller expects 150 BBTU of make-up in year 2 (900 current) and the rest to be taken or to expire later (1,500 non-current). A seller that must hand unclaimed amounts to a government records a liability for them, not breakage revenue (para. B47).</p><p>The shortfall payment would be a refund liability, not deferred revenue, if the contract obliged the seller to return cash for gas never taken. PHE's policy says the balance becomes revenue at expiry, so it reads as an obligation to deliver gas.</p><h2>6. Customer options, service warranties and upfront fees</h2><p>Some contract liabilities arise without any prepayment, because part of today's price pays for something promised later.</p><p><strong>Customer options.</strong> An option to buy more goods later is a separate performance obligation only if it gives a <em>material right</em>, something the customer would not get without the contract, such as a discount beyond what other customers receive (paras. B39–B40). For example (illustration), a buyer contracts for 1,000 BBTU at 6 (6,000) and receives an option to buy another 100 BBTU next year at 4.5, a discount not offered to anyone else. The seller expects 80 percent take-up.</p><blockquote><p>SSP of the option = (6.0 − 4.5) × 100 × 80% = 120</p><p>Gas = 6,000 × 6,000 / 6,120 = 5,882.35; option = 6,000 × 120 / 6,120 = 117.65</p><p>On delivery: Dr Cash 6,000 / Cr Revenue 5,882.35 / Cr Contract liability 117.65</p></blockquote><p>The 117.65 becomes revenue when the option is exercised or lapses.</p><p><strong>Warranties.</strong> The lecture note for Ch 18 separates two kinds. An <em>assurance-type</em> warranty promises only that the product works as agreed; it is not a performance obligation and its expected cost is a provision under PSAK 237 (see the Decommissioning provision essay for provisions). A <em>service-type</em> warranty, sold separately or covering more than agreed specifications, is a performance obligation. For example (illustration), a compressor package sells for 1,050 with a two-year extended service plan; SSPs are 1,000 and 100.</p><blockquote><p>Service plan = 1,050 × 100 / 1,100 = 95.45, held as a contract liability and released over two years</p></blockquote><p><strong>Upfront non-refundable fees.</strong> A connection fee, a joining fee or a franchise's initial fee usually pays in advance for the future goods or services, so it is deferred over the period they are supplied (paras. B48–B51). Ch 18's franchise section is the classic case.</p><h2>7. Deferred revenue across industries</h2><p><strong>Subscriptions and prepaid telecom.</strong> Prepaid mobile credit is a contract liability until the subscriber uses it, and expired credit is breakage. For example (illustration), an operator sells 1,000 of prepaid credit and its history says 8 percent will expire unused, so expected use is 920.</p><blockquote><p>When 460 has been used: revenue = 460 + 80 × (460 / 920) = 460 + 40 = 500; liability = 1,000 − 500 = 500</p></blockquote><p><strong>Airlines.</strong> Tickets sold for future flights sit in a liability, often called unearned transportation revenue, until the flight. Frequent-flyer miles awarded with a ticket are a material right. For example (illustration), a ticket sells for 300 and earns miles with an SSP of 15.</p><blockquote><p>Ticket = 300 × 300 / 315 = 285.71, released when flown; miles = 300 × 15 / 315 = 14.29, released when redeemed or when redemption becomes remote</p></blockquote><p><strong>Real estate pre-sales.</strong> Indonesian developers collect booking fees, down payments and instalments long before handover, so contract liabilities can be large on their balance sheets. Whether revenue comes at handover or over construction depends on para. 35(c): an apartment unit has no alternative use once sold, but revenue is over time only if the developer has an enforceable right to payment for work done to date, which turns on the sale agreement and local law. The gap between payment and handover is usually long enough for a significant financing component. For example (illustration), a buyer pays 1,000 three years before handover and the developer's borrowing rate is 8 percent.</p><blockquote><p>Liability at handover = 1,000 × 1.08³ = 1,259.71; revenue at handover 1,259.71; interest expense of 259.71 accreted on the liability over the three years</p></blockquote><p><strong>Construction and engineering.</strong> Billings ahead of progress give a contract liability, progress ahead of billings a contract asset, netted per contract (para. 105). The Trade receivables essay works the cost-to-cost example.</p><p><strong>Government grants are not contract liabilities.</strong> A grant related to an asset may be presented as <em>deferred income</em> under PSAK 220 (formerly PSAK 61, IAS 20), but the government is not a customer receiving goods or services, so PSAK 115 does not apply.</p><h2>8. Presentation and analysis: PHE's Note 24</h2><p>PHE splits the balance into take-or-pay gas and others, each with a current and non-current portion (Note 24, Daftar 5/118, PDF p. 135).</p><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>31 Dec 2024</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>31 Dec 2023</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Take-or-pay gas, current</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>4,718</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>1,332</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Others, current</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>1,294</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2,794</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>Current portion</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>6,012</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>4,126</strong></p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Take-or-pay gas, non-current</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>10,800</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>3,898</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Others, non-current</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2,318</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>3,119</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>Non-current portion</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>13,118</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>7,017</strong></p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>Total deferred revenue</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>19,130</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>11,143</strong></p></td></tr></tbody></table><blockquote><p>Growth in total = 19,130 / 11,143 − 1 = 71.7%</p><p>Take-or-pay gas = 4,718 + 10,800 = 15,518 (2024); 1,332 + 3,898 = 5,230 (2023), 2.97 times the 2023 balance</p><p>Current share = 6,012 / 19,130 = 31.4%</p><p>Total deferred revenue / revenue = 19,130 / 14,330,340 = 0.13%</p></blockquote><p>The take-or-pay balance rose by 15,518 − 5,230 = 10,288; at section 5's illustrative 6 per BBTU that would be about 1,715 BBTU of unrecovered shortfall. In the mechanics of section 5 the rise is consistent with buyers taking less than their minimum quantities in 2024, while "others" fell. Two-thirds sits in non-current, consistent with make-up gas, or expiry, expected after 2025.</p><p>Note 24 gives the balances at both year-ends, which meets the first part of para. 116. It does not give the revenue recognised from the opening balance (para. 116(b)) or explain the change (para. 118). Para. 120's remaining-obligation disclosure can be skipped for sales billed per unit delivered (para. 121(b)). At 0.13 percent of revenue the omissions are immaterial under PSAK 201, but they are why a reader cannot tell how much of the 11,143 at the start of 2024 became revenue, how much was added, or how much is breakage at expiry.</p><p>PHE also presents deferred revenue as a financial liability. Its policy lists it among liabilities classified as loans and borrowings (Note 3, Daftar 5/44, PDF p. 61), a label from PSAK 55, which PSAK 109 replaced; it appears among other financial liabilities in Note 47 (Daftar 5/222, PDF p. 239); and the PSAK 107 liquidity table shows the 19,130 as a contractual outflow, 6,012 within one year and 13,118 in one to five years (Daftar 5/233, PDF p. 250). Under PSAK 232 (formerly PSAK 50, IAS 32), deferred revenue is not a financial liability because it is settled by delivering goods or services, not cash (application guidance AG11). That is the lecture note's line between deposits and unearned revenue from section 1. Since PHE's own policy settles the take-or-pay balance in gas or at expiry, the classification is hard to reconcile with it, though in a line this small it is a presentation inconsistency rather than a material misstatement.</p><p>Note 43e (Daftar 5/155–5/156, PDF pp. 172–173) carries deferred tax on deferred revenue of 2,923 at the end of 2023 (2,884 in the asset schedule, 39 as a deductible item in the liability schedule) and 2,874 at the end of 2024, almost all moved from one schedule to the other (net charge 49). That is 15 percent of the 2024 balance, below any PSC tax rate, so not all of the balance appears to be a temporary difference (see the Deferred tax assets essay).</p><h2>9. Upstream note</h2><p>Take-or-pay protects the investment case for gas fields, whose output must flow to a contracted buyer through dedicated infrastructure. Under a PSC, a gas sales agreement covers both the contractor's and the government's share of the gas. PHE's FS state the policy and the balance but not the contract terms, the make-up period, or whether shortfall payments on the government's share sit in PHE's balance.</p><h2>10. Related lines</h2><ul><li><p><code>deferred-revenue-non-current</code>: the same balance, expected to be released after twelve months.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/revenues">revenues</a>: where the liability is released.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/trade-receivables-net">trade-receivables-net</a>: the unconditional right that a due-date contract liability creates, and contract assets.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/trade-payables">trade-payables</a>: the boundary between obligations settled in cash and those settled in goods.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/decommissioning-site-restoration-provision">decommissioning-site-restoration-provision</a>: where assurance warranties and other provisions belong.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/deferred-tax-assets">deferred-tax-assets</a> and <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/deferred-tax-assets">deferred-tax-liabilities</a>: the tax effect in Note 43e.</p></li></ul><h3>Least-confident assertions</h3><ul><li><p>The reading that PHE's take-or-pay balance follows the remote approach to breakage rests on the phrase "or when the contract expires" in Note 3u. PHE does not state its breakage method, and the contract terms (make-up period, refund clauses) are not disclosed.</p></li><li><p>Classifying deferred revenue as a financial liability conflicts with IAS 32 AG11 only if no part of the balance is refundable in cash. PHE's policy implies it is not, but the "others" component (3,612 at the end of 2024) is not described at all.</p></li><li><p>The reading of the 2024 increase as larger buyer shortfalls follows from the mechanics, and the 1,715 BBTU uses an illustrative price. A new contract with a larger minimum, or a reclassification, would produce the same movement, and PHE gives no roll-forward.</p></li><li><p>That the Note 43e tax balance on deferred revenue implies only part of the balance is a temporary difference is an inference. Different tax rates across PSCs, or balances held in entities that recognise no deferred tax, could also explain it.</p></li><li><p>Applying para. 62(a) to make-up gas assumes the buyer controls the timing of make-up. Contracts that fix a make-up schedule could carry a financing component.</p></li></ul><h2>References</h2><ol><li><p>PT Pertamina Hulu Energi dan Entitas Anak, consolidated financial statements 31 December 2024 (audited, USD thousand): statement of financial position (Daftar 1/2, PDF p. 12); Note 3 financial liabilities (Daftar 5/44, PDF p. 61); Note 3p (Daftar 5/61–5/63, PDF pp. 78–80); Note 3u (Daftar 5/71, PDF p. 88); Note 24 (Daftar 5/118, PDF p. 135); Note 35 (Daftar 5/148, PDF p. 165); Note 43e (Daftar 5/155–5/156, PDF pp. 172–173); financial instruments by category (Daftar 5/222, PDF p. 239); liquidity table (Daftar 5/233, PDF p. 250). VERIFIED (PHE FS 2024, on disk).</p></li><li><p>Lecture notes this series follows, "Inter 2 – Chapter 18 – Revenue Recognition" (Rumpun Akuntansi Keuangan): asset-liability approach, five steps, significant financing component, allocation by SSP, over time and point in time, warranties, franchises. NOTES. The notes treat the financing component only for deferred payment; the prepayment case in section 3 is this essay's extension. Breakage, take-or-pay and material rights are not in the notes; sections 5 and 6 follow PSAK 115's application guidance.</p></li><li><p>Lecture notes, "Inter 2 – Chapter 13 – Current Liabilities, Provisions and Contingencies": customer advances and deposits, unearned revenue (10,000 received 2 December, goods arriving 5 December). NOTES.</p></li><li><p>PSAK 115 <em>Pendapatan dari Kontrak dengan Pelanggan</em> (IFRS 15). Paras. 35, 38, 55, 62–64, 105–109, 116, 118, 120–121, B40, B42, B44–B49 VERIFIED (web, IFRS 15 as adopted, Commission Regulation (EU) 2016/1905, legislation.gov.uk). Paras. 60–61, B39, B50–B51 STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED. IASB/FASB Transition Resource Group, July 2015, Agenda Paper 43 <em>Commodities</em> (point in time vs over time for commodity deliveries). VERIFIED (web, ifrs.org; PwC Viewpoint TRG summary).</p></li><li><p>PSAK 201 (IAS 1), para. 69 (current liabilities) and materiality. Number VERIFIED (web, IAI renumbering list); content STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.</p></li><li><p>PSAK 232 <em>Instrumen Keuangan: Penyajian</em> (IAS 32), AG11 (deferred revenue is not a financial liability). VERIFIED (web, NWB reproduction of IAS 32 AL11; HMRC Corporate Finance Manual CFM21130, secondary).</p></li><li><p>PSAK 237 (IAS 37), PSAK 220 (IAS 20), PSAK 107 (IFRS 7), PSAK 55 (IAS 39, superseded by PSAK 109): numbers VERIFIED (web, IAI renumbering list, as in sibling essays); content STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.</p></li><li><p>Kieso, Weygandt and Warfield, <em>Intermediate Accounting</em>, IFRS edition, chs. 13 and 18, the textbook the lecture notes summarise. Not on disk; NOT VERIFIED.</p></li></ol>$c$,
       content_json = $j${"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"In brief","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Deferred revenue is a contract liability, an obligation to transfer goods or services for which the customer has already paid or for which payment is already due. It is recognised at the earlier of the two and released to revenue as each performance obligation is satisfied.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The amount in the liability depends on the five steps of PSAK 115. Allocation decides how much of a payment belongs to undelivered obligations, a significant financing component can make the liability grow with interest, and customer options and service warranties create obligations that must be deferred even when nothing was prepaid for them.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Rights that customers pay for but never use, such as unused make-up gas under a take-or-pay contract, are breakage. They become revenue in proportion to the rights customers do use or, when the seller does not expect to be entitled to breakage, only once use becomes remote.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"horizontalRule"},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PT Pertamina Hulu Energi and subsidiaries (PHE) report deferred revenue, current maturities, of 6,012 at 31 December 2024 against 4,126 a year earlier, with a non-current portion of 13,118 against 7,017, in USD thousand (PHE FS 2024, statement of financial position, Daftar 1/2, PDF p. 12; \"Daftar\" is the printed page). The current line is 0.10 percent of current liabilities (0.07 percent a year earlier), and the two lines together, 19,130, are 0.12 percent of total liabilities (0.07 percent a year earlier) and 0.06 percent of total assets.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"After reading, a student should be able to say when a customer payment becomes a liability, measure it through the five steps, release it with journals, and recognise the same mechanics in airlines, telecoms and property developers.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"All amounts are in USD thousand unless stated. Figures from PHE's statements are cited to their note. Every other number is labelled an illustration. The governing standard is PSAK 115 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Pendapatan dari Kontrak dengan Pelanggan","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (formerly PSAK 72, IFRS 15), and PHE's notes use the new number.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1. What deferred revenue is","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cash received before performance is owed back in goods or services, so it is a liability. Kieso's older vocabulary calls it ","type":"text"},{"text":"unearned revenue","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" and lists it among current liabilities. The lecture note for Ch 13 records it the classic way: 10,000 received on 2 December for goods shipped FOB destination is credited to unearned revenue, and moves to sales revenue when the goods arrive on 5 December. The same note keeps ","type":"text"},{"text":"customer advances and deposits","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" apart. A returnable deposit is owed back in cash, while unearned revenue is owed in goods or services, a line that returns in section 8.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 115 keeps the idea but rebuilds the reasoning. Its ","type":"text"},{"text":"asset-liability approach","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" recognises revenue when a contract asset rises or a contract liability falls, rather than when revenue is \"earned and realised\". Three balances follow.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Balance","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"What it is","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"When it arises","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Receivable","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"An unconditional right to consideration: only the passage of time is needed before payment is due (para. 108)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Performance done, or payment due under a non-cancellable contract","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Contract asset","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A right to consideration conditional on something other than time, usually further performance (para. 107)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Performance done ahead of the right to bill","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Contract liability","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"An obligation to transfer goods or services for which consideration has been received or is due (para. 106)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Payment received or due ahead of performance","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The Trade receivables essay teaches the first two rows; this essay is about the third.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The contract liability is recognised \"when the payment is made or the payment is due (whichever is earlier)\" (para. 106). A payment that is due but unpaid creates the liability too, if the contract cannot be cancelled. For example (illustration), a PHE drilling subsidiary signs a non-cancellable contract on 1 October 2024 to drill one well and then monitor it for twelve months, for 3,000, invoiced on 1 November and payable on 1 December. On 1 December nothing has been paid and no drilling has started.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Non-cancellable, payment due: Dr Trade receivables 3,000 / Cr Contract liability 3,000","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cancellable until drilling starts: no entry, because the right to payment is not yet unconditional","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Each contract is presented as one net position, a contract asset or a contract liability, never both (para. 105). PSAK 115 allows other names as long as receivables stay distinguishable (para. 109), which is why PHE and most Indonesian issuers still say ","type":"text"},{"text":"pendapatan tangguhan","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", deferred revenue.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2. The five steps and where the liability comes from","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Every contract liability is a product of the five steps that open the lecture note for Inter 2 Ch 18.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"orderedList","attrs":{"type":null,"start":1},"content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Identify the contract with the customer.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Identify the ","type":"text"},{"text":"performance obligations","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", the promises to transfer a distinct good or service.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Determine the ","type":"text"},{"text":"transaction price","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", the consideration the entity expects to be entitled to.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Allocate the price to the obligations in proportion to their ","type":"text"},{"text":"standalone selling prices","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (SSP), the prices at which each would sell on its own.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Recognise revenue when, or as, each obligation is satisfied by transferring control.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The contract liability is the part of consideration received or due that step 4 has assigned to obligations step 5 has not yet satisfied. In the drilling contract, drilling and monitoring are distinct, since a customer could buy either from another contractor. Suppose drilling alone would sell for 2,700 and a year of monitoring for 900 (illustration).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Total SSP = 2,700 + 900 = 3,600","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Drilling = 3,000 × 2,700 / 3,600 = 2,250","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Monitoring = 3,000 × 900 / 3,600 = 750","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The 600 bundle discount is spread across both obligations in proportion, unless there is evidence it belongs to one of them. The customer pays the 3,000 on 5 December. Drilling runs through December on the customer's well, so the customer controls the well as it is drilled and the obligation is satisfied over time; by 31 December the well is finished.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"5 December: Dr Cash 3,000 / Cr Trade receivables 3,000","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"31 December: Dr Contract liability 2,250 / Cr Revenue, drilling services 2,250","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The liability left at the year end is 750, all current because monitoring runs through 2025. Had the price been allocated by invoice line instead of SSP, with the contract billing 2,800 for drilling and 200 for monitoring, the year-end liability would be 200 and 550 of revenue would have been pulled into 2024. Step 4 exists to stop that.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"3. The transaction price: financing, refunds and rebates","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The transaction price is not always the cash received, and the differences flow into the liability.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Significant financing component.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" When payment and performance are far apart, part of the payment is interest. If the customer pays early, the seller is borrowing from the customer, so the seller accretes interest on the contract liability and recognises more revenue than the cash received (paras. 60–65). The rate is the one a separate financing transaction between the two parties would carry, reflecting the credit of whoever receives the financing, here the seller (para. 64). For example (illustration), an industrial buyer prepays 10,000 on 1 January 2024 for gas a PHE subsidiary will deliver in full at the end of 2025, and the subsidiary could borrow for two years at 6 percent.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Year 1 interest = 10,000 × 6% = 600; liability = 10,600","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Year 2 interest = 10,600 × 6% = 636; liability = 11,236","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Financing component recognised","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"No financing adjustment (if para. 62(a) or 63 applied)","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Receipt","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Cash 10,000 / Cr Contract liability 10,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Cash 10,000 / Cr Contract liability 10,000","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2024","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Interest expense 600 / Cr Contract liability 600","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"no entry","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2025","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Interest expense 636 / Cr Contract liability 636","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"no entry","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Delivery","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Contract liability 11,236 / Cr Revenue 11,236","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Contract liability 10,000 / Cr Revenue 10,000","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Revenue less finance cost is 10,000 either way, but the left column shows 11,236 of revenue and 1,236 of finance cost, which is what a buyer paying the cash price at delivery would produce plus a separate loan. In this fact pattern, with a fixed delivery date two years out, neither exception applies, but they cover many common cases. No adjustment is needed when, at inception, the gap between payment and transfer is expected to be one year or less (para. 63), and there is no significant financing component when the customer prepaid and itself decides when the goods are transferred (para. 62(a)). The second exception is the one that applies to take-or-pay make-up gas in section 5.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Refund liabilities.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Consideration the seller expects to give back in cash is not transaction price at all. It is a ","type":"text"},{"text":"refund liability","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (para. 55), a separate liability settled in cash rather than in goods. For example (illustration), a buyer pays 5,000 for gas and will receive a 2 percent volume rebate in cash, which the seller expects it to earn.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Cash 5,000 / Cr Revenue 4,900 / Cr Refund liability 100","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Only an obligation to deliver goods or services is a contract liability. The distinction comes back in section 8, because PHE's classification blurs it.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"4. Releasing the liability: point in time or over time","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The liability falls when control passes, and control passes in one of two ways. An obligation is satisfied ","type":"text"},{"text":"over time","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" if the customer receives and consumes the benefit as the entity performs, if the customer controls the asset as it is created, or if the asset has no alternative use to the seller and the seller has an enforceable right to payment for work done to date (para. 35). Otherwise it is satisfied ","type":"text"},{"text":"at a point in time","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", judged by indicators such as a present right to payment, legal title, physical possession, transfer of risks and rewards, and the customer's acceptance (para. 38).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"For over-time obligations the entity picks a ","type":"text"},{"text":"measure of progress","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":". ","type":"text"},{"text":"Output methods","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" count what has been delivered (months of service, metres drilled, BBTU of gas; one BBTU is a billion British thermal units). ","type":"text"},{"text":"Input methods","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" count effort, such as cost incurred against total expected cost, Kieso's cost-to-cost method. Monitoring is a stand-ready service delivered evenly, so time elapsed is the natural output measure.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Monthly release = 750 / 12 = 62.5","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Each month of 2025: Dr Contract liability 62.5 / Cr Revenue, monitoring services 62.5","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Gas delivered through a pipeline meter is released quantity by quantity. Whether that is a series of point-in-time sales or one obligation satisfied over time depends on whether the buyer consumes the gas as it arrives (IASB/FASB Transition Resource Group, July 2015). PHE's policy treats goods as generally transferred at a point in time and recognises crude and gas revenue \"based on the actual lifting amount and sold to customers\" (Note 3p, Daftar 5/62, PDF p. 79). Either way a prepayment for gas is released as the gas is delivered.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"5. Take-or-pay and breakage","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A take-or-pay clause makes the buyer pay for a minimum quantity whether or not it takes it. Such contracts typically soften it with a ","type":"text"},{"text":"make-up right","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":": the buyer may take the gas it paid for but did not take in later years, usually only after taking that year's own minimum. PHE's policy describes this balance: deferred revenue is \"amounts billed and collected involving 'take or pay' gas quantities, which will be recognized as revenue when the related gas quantity are delivered to customers or when the contract expires\" (Note 3u, Daftar 5/71, PDF p. 88).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Take a contract of the kind behind PHE's line (illustration). A subsidiary sells gas at USD 6 per MMBtu, which is 6 per BBTU in USD thousand. The annual contract quantity is 2,000 BBTU and the take-or-pay level is 80 percent, so 1,600 BBTU. Make-up gas may be taken in the two years after the shortfall.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Year 1: buyer takes 1,200 BBTU; revenue = 1,200 × 6 = 7,200","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Shortfall = 1,600 − 1,200 = 400 BBTU, billed and paid = 400 × 6 = 2,400","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Cash 2,400 / Cr Contract liability, take-or-pay 2,400","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The 2,400 is not revenue in year 1. The buyer has paid for gas it can still demand, so the seller owes 400 BBTU. The buyer decides when to take it, so para. 62(a) removes any financing component. Without a make-up right the shortfall payment would be revenue in year 1, because the seller would owe nothing more for it; the make-up right is what creates the liability.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Year 2: buyer takes 1,750 BBTU; 1,600 is year 2's own minimum, paid in cash (9,600); 150 BBTU is make-up","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Contract liability 900 / Cr Revenue 900 (150 × 6)","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Year 3: buyer takes exactly 1,600 BBTU; 250 BBTU of make-up remains unused when the right expires","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The unused 250 BBTU is ","type":"text"},{"text":"breakage","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":": a right the customer paid for and never exercised. PSAK 115 gives two treatments (paras. B44–B47). If the seller expects to be entitled to breakage, it recognises the expected amount in proportion to the pattern of rights the customer exercises. If it does not, it waits until the likelihood of the customer exercising its remaining rights becomes remote. The expected amount is subject to the constraint on variable consideration, so the seller must be confident it will not reverse. Suppose the seller's history shows that 40 percent of make-up gas is never taken (illustration).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Expected make-up taken = 400 × 60% = 240 BBTU; expected breakage = 160 × 6 = 960","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Year 2 share of expected use = 150 / 240 = 62.5%; breakage recognised = 960 × 62.5% = 600","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Year 3 remainder at expiry = 2,400 − 900 − 600 = 900","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Revenue from the 2,400","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Remote approach (PHE's policy)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Proportional approach","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Year 2","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"900","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"900 + 600 = 1,500","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Year 3, at expiry","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1,500","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"900","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Total","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2,400","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2,400","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Both release the same 2,400. The proportional approach brings breakage forward when the seller has evidence for it; the remote approach waits. PHE's wording, recognition \"when the contract expires\", fits the remote approach at its latest point and is not the proportional approach.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The current and non-current split follows the same schedule. A contract liability is current to the extent the seller expects to satisfy it within twelve months (PSAK 201.69, formerly PSAK 1, IAS 1), and it is settled by delivery or expiry, not cash. At the end of year 1, under the remote approach, the seller expects 150 BBTU of make-up in year 2 (900 current) and the rest to be taken or to expire later (1,500 non-current). A seller that must hand unclaimed amounts to a government records a liability for them, not breakage revenue (para. B47).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The shortfall payment would be a refund liability, not deferred revenue, if the contract obliged the seller to return cash for gas never taken. PHE's policy says the balance becomes revenue at expiry, so it reads as an obligation to deliver gas.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"6. Customer options, service warranties and upfront fees","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Some contract liabilities arise without any prepayment, because part of today's price pays for something promised later.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Customer options.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" An option to buy more goods later is a separate performance obligation only if it gives a ","type":"text"},{"text":"material right","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", something the customer would not get without the contract, such as a discount beyond what other customers receive (paras. B39–B40). For example (illustration), a buyer contracts for 1,000 BBTU at 6 (6,000) and receives an option to buy another 100 BBTU next year at 4.5, a discount not offered to anyone else. The seller expects 80 percent take-up.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"SSP of the option = (6.0 − 4.5) × 100 × 80% = 120","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Gas = 6,000 × 6,000 / 6,120 = 5,882.35; option = 6,000 × 120 / 6,120 = 117.65","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"On delivery: Dr Cash 6,000 / Cr Revenue 5,882.35 / Cr Contract liability 117.65","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The 117.65 becomes revenue when the option is exercised or lapses.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Warranties.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" The lecture note for Ch 18 separates two kinds. An ","type":"text"},{"text":"assurance-type","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" warranty promises only that the product works as agreed; it is not a performance obligation and its expected cost is a provision under PSAK 237 (see the Decommissioning provision essay for provisions). A ","type":"text"},{"text":"service-type","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" warranty, sold separately or covering more than agreed specifications, is a performance obligation. For example (illustration), a compressor package sells for 1,050 with a two-year extended service plan; SSPs are 1,000 and 100.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Service plan = 1,050 × 100 / 1,100 = 95.45, held as a contract liability and released over two years","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Upfront non-refundable fees.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" A connection fee, a joining fee or a franchise's initial fee usually pays in advance for the future goods or services, so it is deferred over the period they are supplied (paras. B48–B51). Ch 18's franchise section is the classic case.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"7. Deferred revenue across industries","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Subscriptions and prepaid telecom.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Prepaid mobile credit is a contract liability until the subscriber uses it, and expired credit is breakage. For example (illustration), an operator sells 1,000 of prepaid credit and its history says 8 percent will expire unused, so expected use is 920.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"When 460 has been used: revenue = 460 + 80 × (460 / 920) = 460 + 40 = 500; liability = 1,000 − 500 = 500","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Airlines.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Tickets sold for future flights sit in a liability, often called unearned transportation revenue, until the flight. Frequent-flyer miles awarded with a ticket are a material right. For example (illustration), a ticket sells for 300 and earns miles with an SSP of 15.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Ticket = 300 × 300 / 315 = 285.71, released when flown; miles = 300 × 15 / 315 = 14.29, released when redeemed or when redemption becomes remote","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Real estate pre-sales.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Indonesian developers collect booking fees, down payments and instalments long before handover, so contract liabilities can be large on their balance sheets. Whether revenue comes at handover or over construction depends on para. 35(c): an apartment unit has no alternative use once sold, but revenue is over time only if the developer has an enforceable right to payment for work done to date, which turns on the sale agreement and local law. The gap between payment and handover is usually long enough for a significant financing component. For example (illustration), a buyer pays 1,000 three years before handover and the developer's borrowing rate is 8 percent.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Liability at handover = 1,000 × 1.08³ = 1,259.71; revenue at handover 1,259.71; interest expense of 259.71 accreted on the liability over the three years","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Construction and engineering.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Billings ahead of progress give a contract liability, progress ahead of billings a contract asset, netted per contract (para. 105). The Trade receivables essay works the cost-to-cost example.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Government grants are not contract liabilities.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" A grant related to an asset may be presented as ","type":"text"},{"text":"deferred income","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" under PSAK 220 (formerly PSAK 61, IAS 20), but the government is not a customer receiving goods or services, so PSAK 115 does not apply.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"8. Presentation and analysis: PHE's Note 24","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PHE splits the balance into take-or-pay gas and others, each with a current and non-current portion (Note 24, Daftar 5/118, PDF p. 135).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"31 Dec 2024","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"31 Dec 2023","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Take-or-pay gas, current","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"4,718","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1,332","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Others, current","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1,294","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2,794","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Current portion","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"6,012","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"4,126","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Take-or-pay gas, non-current","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"10,800","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"3,898","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Others, non-current","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2,318","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"3,119","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Non-current portion","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"13,118","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"7,017","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Total deferred revenue","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"19,130","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"11,143","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Growth in total = 19,130 / 11,143 − 1 = 71.7%","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Take-or-pay gas = 4,718 + 10,800 = 15,518 (2024); 1,332 + 3,898 = 5,230 (2023), 2.97 times the 2023 balance","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Current share = 6,012 / 19,130 = 31.4%","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Total deferred revenue / revenue = 19,130 / 14,330,340 = 0.13%","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The take-or-pay balance rose by 15,518 − 5,230 = 10,288; at section 5's illustrative 6 per BBTU that would be about 1,715 BBTU of unrecovered shortfall. In the mechanics of section 5 the rise is consistent with buyers taking less than their minimum quantities in 2024, while \"others\" fell. Two-thirds sits in non-current, consistent with make-up gas, or expiry, expected after 2025.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Note 24 gives the balances at both year-ends, which meets the first part of para. 116. It does not give the revenue recognised from the opening balance (para. 116(b)) or explain the change (para. 118). Para. 120's remaining-obligation disclosure can be skipped for sales billed per unit delivered (para. 121(b)). At 0.13 percent of revenue the omissions are immaterial under PSAK 201, but they are why a reader cannot tell how much of the 11,143 at the start of 2024 became revenue, how much was added, or how much is breakage at expiry.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PHE also presents deferred revenue as a financial liability. Its policy lists it among liabilities classified as loans and borrowings (Note 3, Daftar 5/44, PDF p. 61), a label from PSAK 55, which PSAK 109 replaced; it appears among other financial liabilities in Note 47 (Daftar 5/222, PDF p. 239); and the PSAK 107 liquidity table shows the 19,130 as a contractual outflow, 6,012 within one year and 13,118 in one to five years (Daftar 5/233, PDF p. 250). Under PSAK 232 (formerly PSAK 50, IAS 32), deferred revenue is not a financial liability because it is settled by delivering goods or services, not cash (application guidance AG11). That is the lecture note's line between deposits and unearned revenue from section 1. Since PHE's own policy settles the take-or-pay balance in gas or at expiry, the classification is hard to reconcile with it, though in a line this small it is a presentation inconsistency rather than a material misstatement.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Note 43e (Daftar 5/155–5/156, PDF pp. 172–173) carries deferred tax on deferred revenue of 2,923 at the end of 2023 (2,884 in the asset schedule, 39 as a deductible item in the liability schedule) and 2,874 at the end of 2024, almost all moved from one schedule to the other (net charge 49). That is 15 percent of the 2024 balance, below any PSC tax rate, so not all of the balance appears to be a temporary difference (see the Deferred tax assets essay).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"9. Upstream note","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Take-or-pay protects the investment case for gas fields, whose output must flow to a contracted buyer through dedicated infrastructure. Under a PSC, a gas sales agreement covers both the contractor's and the government's share of the gas. PHE's FS state the policy and the balance but not the contract terms, the make-up period, or whether shortfall payments on the government's share sit in PHE's balance.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"10. Related lines","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"deferred-revenue-non-current","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"text":": the same balance, expected to be released after twelve months.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"revenues","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/revenues","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": where the liability is released.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"trade-receivables-net","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/trade-receivables-net","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": the unconditional right that a due-date contract liability creates, and contract assets.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"trade-payables","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/trade-payables","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": the boundary between obligations settled in cash and those settled in goods.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"decommissioning-site-restoration-provision","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/decommissioning-site-restoration-provision","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": where assurance warranties and other provisions belong.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"deferred-tax-assets","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/deferred-tax-assets","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":" and ","type":"text"},{"text":"deferred-tax-liabilities","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/deferred-tax-assets","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": the tax effect in Note 43e.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Least-confident assertions","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The reading that PHE's take-or-pay balance follows the remote approach to breakage rests on the phrase \"or when the contract expires\" in Note 3u. PHE does not state its breakage method, and the contract terms (make-up period, refund clauses) are not disclosed.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Classifying deferred revenue as a financial liability conflicts with IAS 32 AG11 only if no part of the balance is refundable in cash. PHE's policy implies it is not, but the \"others\" component (3,612 at the end of 2024) is not described at all.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The reading of the 2024 increase as larger buyer shortfalls follows from the mechanics, and the 1,715 BBTU uses an illustrative price. A new contract with a larger minimum, or a reclassification, would produce the same movement, and PHE gives no roll-forward.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"That the Note 43e tax balance on deferred revenue implies only part of the balance is a temporary difference is an inference. Different tax rates across PSCs, or balances held in entities that recognise no deferred tax, could also explain it.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Applying para. 62(a) to make-up gas assumes the buyer controls the timing of make-up. Contracts that fix a make-up schedule could carry a financing component.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"References","type":"text"}]},{"type":"orderedList","attrs":{"type":null,"start":1},"content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PT Pertamina Hulu Energi dan Entitas Anak, consolidated financial statements 31 December 2024 (audited, USD thousand): statement of financial position (Daftar 1/2, PDF p. 12); Note 3 financial liabilities (Daftar 5/44, PDF p. 61); Note 3p (Daftar 5/61–5/63, PDF pp. 78–80); Note 3u (Daftar 5/71, PDF p. 88); Note 24 (Daftar 5/118, PDF p. 135); Note 35 (Daftar 5/148, PDF p. 165); Note 43e (Daftar 5/155–5/156, PDF pp. 172–173); financial instruments by category (Daftar 5/222, PDF p. 239); liquidity table (Daftar 5/233, PDF p. 250). VERIFIED (PHE FS 2024, on disk).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Lecture notes this series follows, \"Inter 2 – Chapter 18 – Revenue Recognition\" (Rumpun Akuntansi Keuangan): asset-liability approach, five steps, significant financing component, allocation by SSP, over time and point in time, warranties, franchises. NOTES. The notes treat the financing component only for deferred payment; the prepayment case in section 3 is this essay's extension. Breakage, take-or-pay and material rights are not in the notes; sections 5 and 6 follow PSAK 115's application guidance.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Lecture notes, \"Inter 2 – Chapter 13 – Current Liabilities, Provisions and Contingencies\": customer advances and deposits, unearned revenue (10,000 received 2 December, goods arriving 5 December). NOTES.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 115 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Pendapatan dari Kontrak dengan Pelanggan","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (IFRS 15). Paras. 35, 38, 55, 62–64, 105–109, 116, 118, 120–121, B40, B42, B44–B49 VERIFIED (web, IFRS 15 as adopted, Commission Regulation (EU) 2016/1905, legislation.gov.uk). Paras. 60–61, B39, B50–B51 STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED. IASB/FASB Transition Resource Group, July 2015, Agenda Paper 43 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Commodities","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (point in time vs over time for commodity deliveries). VERIFIED (web, ifrs.org; PwC Viewpoint TRG summary).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 201 (IAS 1), para. 69 (current liabilities) and materiality. Number VERIFIED (web, IAI renumbering list); content STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 232 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Instrumen Keuangan: Penyajian","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (IAS 32), AG11 (deferred revenue is not a financial liability). VERIFIED (web, NWB reproduction of IAS 32 AL11; HMRC Corporate Finance Manual CFM21130, secondary).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 237 (IAS 37), PSAK 220 (IAS 20), PSAK 107 (IFRS 7), PSAK 55 (IAS 39, superseded by PSAK 109): numbers VERIFIED (web, IAI renumbering list, as in sibling essays); content STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Kieso, Weygandt and Warfield, ","type":"text"},{"text":"Intermediate Accounting","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", IFRS edition, chs. 13 and 18, the textbook the lecture notes summarise. Not on disk; NOT VERIFIED.","type":"text"}]}]}]}]}$j$::jsonb,
       presentation = $p${"deck":"The theory of customer prepayments under PSAK 115 from the five steps to disclosure, worked through PHE's take-or-pay gas balances and set beside the industries that collect before they deliver","genre":null,"author_bio":null,"references":[],"hero_caption":null,"key_takeaways":["Deferred revenue is a contract liability, an obligation to transfer goods or services for which the customer has already paid or for which payment is already due. It is recognised at the earlier of the two and released to revenue as each performance obligation is satisfied.","The amount in the liability depends on the five steps of PSAK 115. Allocation decides how much of a payment belongs to undelivered obligations, a significant financing component can make the liability grow with interest, and customer options and service warranties create obligations that must be deferred even when nothing was prepaid for them.","Rights that customers pay for but never use, such as unused make-up gas under a take-or-pay contract, are breakage. They become revenue in proportion to the rights customers do use or, when the seller does not expect to be entitled to breakage, only once use becomes remote."],"revision_note":null}$p$::jsonb,
       updated_at = now()
 WHERE id = '4af0724c-712f-4fa7-8cff-9e640cf00627';
